/* Chapter 2 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":2,"title":"Distinguishing Features of the Islamic Economic System","part":"part-i","partRoman":"I","pages":[21,42],"minutes":45,"estimates":{"f1":45,"f2":55},"difficulty":"Foundational","difficultyAlt":"foundation","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch2-1","text":"Define Shari’ah, its branches and its sources (primary and secondary).","origin":["f1","f2"],"altText":"Define Shari'ah and list its primary and secondary sources"},{"id":"lo-ch2-2","text":"List the primary and secondary objectives (Maqasid) of Shari’ah.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-3","text":"Explain why Islamic economics is studied and the role of Muslim economists past and present.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-4","text":"Distinguish positive from normative economics and explain where Islamic economics fits.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-5","text":"Compare the factors of production and their rewards in capitalism and in Islamic economics.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-6","text":"Explain the Islamic position on individual freedom, the market and State intervention.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-7","text":"State the primary (Maqasid) and secondary objectives of Shari'ah and explain their relevance to economics","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-8","text":"Explain why the author argues Islamic economics should be studied","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-9","text":"Identify key classical Muslim scholars (e.g. Ibn Khaldun, Ibn Taymiyah) and their economic contributions","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-10","text":"Distinguish positive (economics proper) from normative (welfare) economics in the Islamic framework","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-11","text":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch2-12","text":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Islamic finance operates inside the Islamic economic framework. The objectives of Shari’ah, the rule of general permissibility, the treatment of capital (profit with liability, not interest) and the overseeing role of the State explain why later contracts are structured as they are.","whyExpanded":"This chapter lays the conceptual foundation of the Islamic economic worldview — Shari'ah, its objectives, ownership rules, welfare approach, and the State's role — within which Islamic financial institutions must operate. Understanding these fundamentals is essential before studying the specific prohibitions (Chapter 3) and the philosophy of Islamic finance (Chapter 4).","overview":"The chapter lays the foundation for Islamic finance: what Shari’ah is, its sources and objectives; why Islamic economics should be studied; how it relates to positive and normative economics; and its key features — ownership as trust, a welfare approach centred on basic needs, a distinctive theory of factors of production, restrained individual freedom and an overseeing role for the State.","summary":"Islamic finance works inside the Islamic economic system, whose rules derive from the Qur’an and Sunnah, with Ijma‘a, Qiyas and Ijtihad as further sources and General Permissibility governing transactions. Shari’ah seeks human well-being in this world and the Hereafter through six primary objectives and related secondary objectives. Islamic economics is normative, accepts markets and ownership, and seeks equitable distribution and basic-needs fulfilment. Capital earns profit only as a residual and bears loss; rent is for non-consumable assets; the key prohibitions are interest, hazard and gambling. The State facilitates, oversees markets and money, and ensures stakeholders do not exploit each other.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 2 builds the conceptual scaffolding of the Islamic economic system: Shari'ah's definition, sources (Qur'an, Sunnah, Ijma'a, Qiyas via Ijtihad) and its six Primary plus five Secondary Objectives (Maqasid) centred on human welfare (Falah) in this world and the Hereafter. It explains why Islamic economics merits serious study (Islam's integration of worldly and spiritual life, and the demonstrated failure of secular trickle-down economics), surveys classical (especially Ibn Khaldun) and modern contributors, and positions Islamic economics as normative but market-based, aiming for equitable (not equal) distribution. Ownership is framed as a trust from Allah, limited by quality of acquisition rather than quantity; welfare obligations cascade from the individual to relatives to the State; the factors of production differ fundamentally from capitalism in that money-capital cannot earn interest, only risk-contingent profit. The chapter closes by rejecting both unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto-efficiency-based non-intervention, arguing for an active, justice-oriented State role bounded by Shari'ah, with Al-Hisbah as the key market-oversight institution.","summarySection":"2.6","takeaways":["Sources: Qur’an, Sunnah (primary); Ijma‘a, Qiyas via Ijtihad (secondary).","General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah) governs Mu‘amalat.","Maqasid: religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour.","Islamic economics is normative and aims at equitable distribution.","Capital earns profit with liability for loss — never interest; money is not capital per se.","Five factors: land, capital, labour, management, responsibility.","The State oversees markets (Hisbah), ensures justice and stable money.","Shari'ah's primary sources are the Qur'an and Sunnah; secondary sources (Ijma'a, Qiyas) are derived through Ijtihad; socio-economic matters follow the principle of General Permissibility.","The six Primary Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah are protection of religion, life, progeny, property, intellect and honour, giving rise to five Secondary Objectives centred on justice, social security, peace, cooperation and moral values.","Islamic economics integrates worldly and spiritual life and is explicitly normative (concerned with 'what ought to be'), unlike value-neutral positive economics.","Ibn Khaldun is credited as a foundational figure in economic thought, anticipating Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes, and opposing State monopoly/market interference.","Ownership of wealth is a trust from Allah, limited by the lawfulness (quality) of acquisition, not by quantity; welfare/need-fulfilment obligations cascade from individual to family to State.","Money-capital cannot earn interest or rent in Islamic economics; it earns only a residual, risk-contingent 'profit', with the capital owner bearing any loss — a foundational distinction from capitalism's four factors of production.","The most important economic prohibitions are interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar) and gambling (Maisir/Qimar), due to their severe social harm.","The Islamic State plays an active, justice-oriented (not business-running) economic role — rejecting both unrestrained laissez-faire and the Pareto-efficiency assumption that markets self-correct toward equity — with Al-Hisbah as the key oversight institution."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you define Shari’ah and name its branches?","Can you list the primary and secondary sources of Shari’ah?","Can you list the six primary Maqasid and the secondary objectives?","Can you distinguish positive and normative economics?","Can you draw the factors-of-production comparison table?","Can you explain the State’s role and Chapra’s five functions?"],"topics":[{"id":"t2.1","section":"2.1","title":"Introduction: Why Begin with Islamic Economics?","pages":[21,21],"tier":"revision","concepts":["islamic-economics"],"intuition":"Before learning Islamic banking products, why study economics? Because the financial system is supposed to work within an overall economic framework.","simpleExplanation":"This chapter explains the Islamic economic worldview in which Islamic banks and markets operate.","academicExplanation":["Islamic economic principles can promote a balance between social and economic aspects, self and social interests, and between the individual, family, society and State. Although the book’s focus is banking and finance, the author first discusses the framework within which the Islamic financial system is supposed to work."],"examExplanation":"Islamic finance operates within the Islamic economic framework; Ch 2 sets out that worldview.","keyPoints":["Banking and finance are parts of the economic system; their rules stem from the overall framework.","Banking rules stem from the wider economic framework, so the book starts with Islamic economics before finance."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why the economy comes first","kind":"practice","text":"A student who learns only the rules of Murabaha may ask why a bank cannot simply lend at interest. The answer depends on the wider Islamic economic system — its view of money, risk and distribution — which is why the book starts there (p. 21)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t1.7","t2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does the book discuss Islamic economics before Islamic finance?","options":["It is unrelated","Because the Islamic financial system works within the Islamic economic framework","To replace finance topics","Because banks set economic policy"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The rules governing financial institutions stem from the overall economic framework (pp. 21, 41)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t2.2","section":"2.2","title":"Islamic Shari’ah and its Branches","pages":[21,21],"tier":"core","concepts":["shariah"],"intuition":"What exactly is the “Shari’ah” that every Islamic finance contract must follow?","simpleExplanation":"Shari’ah is the code of divine law that guides how people behave individually and collectively. Business and finance contracts must conform to it and help achieve its objectives.","academicExplanation":["Shari’ah refers to a code of law or divine injunctions that regulate human conduct in individual and collective life. All business and financial contracts in Islamic finance must conform to Shari’ah rules with the objective of achieving Maqasid al-Shari’ah. Besides general rules, its specific branches are: Aqaid (belief and worship), Akhlaq (self-discipline), Ahkam (socio-economic and legal systems), Fraidh (obligations) and Nawahi (prohibitions). Islamic economics deals directly or indirectly with all of these."],"examExplanation":"Shari’ah = divine code regulating individual and collective conduct. Branches: Aqaid, Akhlaq, Ahkam, Fraidh, Nawahi. All financial contracts must conform to it and serve its Maqasid.","keyPoints":["Five branches: Aqaid, Akhlaq, Ahkam, Fraidh, Nawahi.","Islamic finance contracts must conform to Shari’ah and help achieve its objectives."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shari’ah","definition":"A code of law or divine injunctions regulating the conduct of human beings in their individual and collective lives."},{"term":"Ahkam","definition":"The branch of Shari’ah dealing with socio-economic and legal systems."}],"examples":[{"title":"Classifying rules by branch","kind":"textbook","text":"Belief in Allah and prayer fall under Aqaid; honesty under Akhlaq; the law of contracts under Ahkam; paying Zakat under Fraidh; the ban on Riba under Nawahi (p. 21)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t2.2.1","t2.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which branch of Shari’ah covers socio-economic and legal systems?","options":["Aqaid","Akhlaq","Ahkam","Nawahi"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Ahkam covers socio-economic and legal systems; Nawahi are prohibitions (p. 21)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.2","sectionTitle":"Islamic Shari'ah and its Objectives / Sources of Shari'ah Tenets","title":"Shari'ah: Definition, Branches and Sources","covers":["t2.2","t2.2.1","t2.2.2"],"overview":"The author defines Shari'ah and its main branches, then explains the hierarchy of sources from which Shari'ah rules are derived.","simpleExplanation":"Shari'ah is the complete code of divine guidance for how Muslims should live, covering beliefs, personal discipline, social/economic rules, duties and prohibitions. Its rules come primarily from the Qur'an and the Prophet's example (Sunnah); when new situations arise that these don't directly address, scholars use consensus (Ijma'a) and reasoned analogy (Qiyas), guided by scholarly effort (Ijtihad).","academicExplanation":"Shari'ah is defined as a code of law / divine injunctions regulating individual and collective human conduct, comprising: Aqaid (matters of belief/worship), Akhlaq (self-discipline), Ahkam (socio-economic and legal systems), Fraidh (obligations) and Nawahi (prohibitions) — Islamic economics engages directly or indirectly with all of these. The primary source is revelation: the Qur'an (believed free from tampering until the Hereafter, per Qur'an 15:9) and the Sunnah (the Prophet's sayings, actions and tacit approvals), whose authority is grounded in verses such as Qur'an 33:21 and 24:54. Secondary sources — Ijma'a (consensus, especially of the Companions) and Qiyas (analogy) — are derived through Ijtihad (the disciplined reasoning of qualified jurists) to address emerging issues; Maslaha-e-Mursalah (general welfare/public interest) and 'Urf (prevalent custom) are additional tools jurists use without compromising the Qur'an/Sunnah. Shari'ah rules split into Dos and Don'ts, and further into rituals (rights of Allah, to be followed strictly) versus socio-economic matters (rights of human beings), the latter governed by the principle of 'General Permissibility' (Ibahatul Asliyah): everything not expressly prohibited is permissible. Lapses regarding Allah's rights may be forgiven by Allah; lapses regarding human rights require forgiveness from the aggrieved party. Accountability for all acts is individual, in both rituals and socio-economic contracts.","examExplanation":"Shari'ah = divine law covering belief, self-discipline, socio-economic/legal rules, obligations and prohibitions. Primary sources: Qur'an and Sunnah (revelation). Secondary sources: Ijma'a (consensus) and Qiyas (analogy), both derived via Ijtihad; also Maslaha-e-Mursalah (public interest) and 'Urf (custom). Key governing principle for socio-economic matters: 'General Permissibility' (Ibahatul Asliyah) — everything not expressly prohibited is permissible. Rights of Allah may be divinely forgiven; rights of fellow humans require the aggrieved party's forgiveness.","keyPoints":["Shari'ah has five branches: Aqaid (belief/worship), Akhlaq (self-discipline), Ahkam (socio-economic/legal systems), Fraidh (obligations), Nawahi (prohibitions).","Primary sources of Shari'ah: the Qur'an and the Sunnah (revelation).","Secondary sources: Ijma'a (consensus, especially of the Companions) and Qiyas (analogy), both derived through Ijtihad (qualified juristic reasoning); Maslaha-e-Mursalah (public interest) and 'Urf (custom) are also tools jurists use.","Socio-economic matters are governed by 'General Permissibility' (Ibahatul Asliyah): whatever is not expressly prohibited is permissible.","Lapses in the rights of Allah may be forgiven by Allah; lapses in the rights of fellow human beings require forgiveness from the aggrieved person(s).","Accountability for one's acts is individual — in both matters of worship and socio-economic contracts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shari'ah","definition":"Divine guidance as given by the Holy Qur'an and the Sunnah of the Prophet Muhammad (pbuh); embodies all aspects of the Islamic faith, including beliefs and practice."},{"term":"Ijma'a","definition":"Consensus — the decision or resolution of the generality of Shari'ah scholars of any time on matters relating to Shari'ah."},{"term":"Qiyas","definition":"Analogy — deriving a rule of law based on an existing law where the underlying reason ('Illah) of the two is the same."},{"term":"Ijtihad","definition":"The mental/juristic effort of a qualified jurist to derive or formulate a rule of law on a matter where revelation is not explicit or certain."},{"term":"Ibahatul Asliyah (General Permissibility)","definition":"The governing principle for socio-economic matters: all acts and things not expressly prohibited by the original sources of Shari'ah are permissible."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah): in socio-economic/Mu'amalat matters, everything is permissible unless expressly prohibited by Shari'ah."],"processSteps":["Consult the Qur'an first as the primary revealed source.","Consult the Sunnah (the Prophet's sayings, actions and tacit approvals) as the second primary source.","Where the Qur'an and Sunnah are not explicit, seek Ijma'a (scholarly consensus).","Where no consensus exists, apply Qiyas (analogy) via Ijtihad, informed also by Maslaha-e-Mursalah (public interest) and 'Urf (custom)."],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Shari'ah (the divine law itself) is sometimes conflated with Fiqh (human juristic interpretation/application of Shari'ah); Chapter 2 focuses on Shari'ah's sources and objectives, not the Fiqh schools in detail."],"distinctions":["Rituals/worship (rights of Allah, followed strictly) vs socio-economic matters (rights of human beings, governed by General Permissibility)."],"relatedConcepts":["Maqasid al-Shari'ah","Ijtihad","Fiqh","Mu'amalat"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2","pages":[21,22]}}],"table":{"caption":"Branches of Shari’ah injunctions (p. 21)","head":["Branch","Covers"],"rows":[["Aqaid","Belief and worship"],["Akhlaq","Self-discipline"],["Ahkam","Socio-economic and legal systems"],["Fraidh","Obligations"],["Nawahi","Prohibitions"]]}},{"id":"t2.2.1","section":"2.2.1","title":"Sources of Shari’ah Tenets","pages":[21,22],"tier":"core","concepts":["shariah","ijtihad","general-permissibility"],"intuition":"Where do the rules for a new financial product come from when the Qur’an does not mention it by name? From a hierarchy of sources and the rule of general permissibility.","simpleExplanation":"The primary sources are the Qur’an and Sunnah. Secondary sources are Ijma‘a (consensus) and Qiyas (analogy), based on Ijtihad (scholarly effort). In business matters, anything not expressly prohibited is allowed.","academicExplanation":["The primary source of divine law is revelation — the Holy Qur’an and the Sunnah (sayings, actions and approvals of the Prophet, pbuh). Secondary sources are Ijma‘a (consensus) and Qiyas (analogy), both based on Ijtihad — the mental effort of scholars with juristic expertise to find solutions to emerging issues in the light of the Qur’an and Sunnah. Maslaha-e-Mursalah (general welfare/interest) and ‘Urf (prevalent practice) are further tools jurists keep in mind, without compromising the basic tenets. Jurists, Shari’ah scholars and Shari’ah boards of Islamic banks issue edicts on this basis.","Shari’ah rules are Dos and Don’ts, divisible into rituals (rights of Allah) and matters disciplining human life (rights of human beings). Rituals must be performed strictly according to the tenets, while socio-economic matters are governed by the rule of General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah): everything not expressly prohibited is permissible. Lapses regarding the rights of human beings can be forgiven only by the aggrieved person, and accountability is individual."],"examExplanation":"Primary: Qur’an, Sunnah. Secondary: Ijma‘a, Qiyas (via Ijtihad). Tools: Maslaha-e-Mursalah, ‘Urf. Socio-economic matters: General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah) — all not expressly prohibited is permitted. Rights of Allah vs rights of human beings.","keyPoints":["Qur’an and Sunnah are the primary sources.","Ijma‘a and Qiyas are secondary sources based on Ijtihad.","Maslaha-e-Mursalah and ‘Urf are additional juristic tools.","Ibahatul Asliyah: permissibility is the general rule in Mu‘amalat.","Violations of human rights can only be forgiven by the aggrieved person.","Maslaha-e-Mursalah (general welfare) and ‘Urf (prevalent practice) are tools jurists use without compromising the Qur’an and Sunnah.","Accountability is individual — in rituals and in contracts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijtihad","definition":"Mental effort of scholars with juristic expertise to find solutions to emerging problems in the light of the Qur’an and Sunnah."},{"term":"Ijma‘a","definition":"Consensus — a secondary source of Shari’ah tenets."},{"term":"Qiyas","definition":"Analogy — finding solutions through analogy with the texts of the Qur’an and Sunnah."},{"term":"Maslaha-e-Mursalah","definition":"General welfare/interest of human beings, used as a juristic tool."},{"term":"‘Urf","definition":"Prevalent practice/custom considered by jurists."},{"term":"Ibahatul Asliyah","definition":"Rule of General Permissibility: all acts and things not expressly prohibited by the original sources are permissible."}],"examples":[{"title":"Ibahatul Asliyah at work","kind":"textbook","text":"No text mentions credit cards or Sukuk. Under the rule of General Permissibility they are allowed unless they involve something prohibited such as Riba or Gharar, which is why scholars examine their features rather than look for express permission (pp. 21–22)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Every business practice must be expressly permitted in the Qur’an to be allowed.","right":"In socio-economic matters the rule is General Permissibility — everything not expressly prohibited is permissible."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5","t2.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What is Ibahatul Asliyah?","options":["The rule that all acts not expressly prohibited are permissible","Consensus of scholars","The prohibition of interest","A type of partnership"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"It is the rule of General Permissibility governing socio-economic matters (p. 22)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Rights of Allah (rituals)","b":"Rights of human beings (Mu‘amalat)","text":"Rituals must be performed strictly per the texts; socio-economic matters follow General Permissibility. Allah may forgive lapses in His rights, but lapses against people are forgiven only by the aggrieved person (p. 22)."}],"table":{"caption":"Hierarchy of sources (as presented in Ch. 2)","head":["Level","Source","Nature"],"rows":[["Primary","Qur’an","Revealed book"],["Primary","Sunnah","Sayings, actions and approvals of the Prophet (pbuh)"],["Secondary","Ijma‘a","Consensus, based on Ijtihad"],["Secondary","Qiyas","Analogy, based on Ijtihad"],["Tools","Maslaha-e-Mursalah, ‘Urf","General welfare and prevalent practice"]]}},{"id":"t2.2.2","section":"2.2.2","title":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari’ah","pages":[22,25],"tier":"core","concepts":["maqasid"],"intuition":"Rules can be followed mechanically. The Maqasid tell you what the rules are for — and help jurists judge new issues.","simpleExplanation":"Shari’ah aims at people’s well-being in this world and the Hereafter. Its primary objectives are protecting religion, life, progeny, property, intellect and honour. These lead to secondary goals such as justice, social security and peace.","academicExplanation":["The study of objectives reflects the spirit of Shari’ah and helps jurists determine permissibility through Ijtihad and Qiyas. The basic objective is the well-being of people in worldly life and the Hereafter, or relieving them of hardship. Wealth belongs to Allah and is held by man as a stewardship; livelihood is necessary but not the only purpose of life. Happiness in the Islamic perspective differs from “pleasure”, the objective of positive economics. The objectives were identified by jurists such as Ghazali, Shatbi and Tahir ibne Ashoor through an inductive survey of the Qur’an and Sunnah.","Primary objectives — protection and preservation of: (1) religion; (2) life (including provision of basic necessities); (3) progeny/family unit; (4) property (valid, Halal earning; discouraging concentration of wealth; hence a comprehensive law of Mu‘amalat); (5) intellect; (6) honour. Secondary objectives: justice and equity; social security, mutual help and solidarity; peace and security; cooperation in goodness and prohibition of evil; and supreme universal moral values including preservation of nature.","Umer Chapra is quoted arguing that if well-being includes humanitarian and spiritual goals, economics must discuss those goals; the material and spiritual aspects are interrelated. Basic socio-economic rights identified from the Qur’an and Sunnah: safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, satisfaction of basic needs, redress, education and a healthy environment. Rulers and regulators must protect the masses from unscrupulous elements."],"examExplanation":"Maqasid = well-being in this world and the Hereafter. Primary: protect religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour. Secondary: justice/equity; social security and mutual help; peace; cooperation in good; universal moral values and nature. Eight socio-economic rights.","keyPoints":["Six primary objectives: religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour.","Five secondary objectives, starting with justice and equity.","Wealth is a stewardship (vicegerency) for which account is to be rendered.","Protection of property underpins the law of Mu‘amalat (transactions).","Eight basic socio-economic rights are listed.","Eight socio-economic rights: safety, information, choice, being heard, basic needs, redress, education and a healthy environment (p. 25).","Objectives were identified inductively by Ghazali, Shatbi and later Tahir ibne Ashoor."],"definitions":[{"term":"Maqasid al-Shari’ah","definition":"The objectives of Shari’ah — the well-being of people in this world and the Hereafter, expressed as primary and secondary objectives."},{"term":"Mu‘amalat","definition":"Transactions among members of society, governed by a comprehensive law protecting property."}],"examples":[{"title":"Chapra’s tests of well-being","kind":"textbook","text":"Chapra suggests goals are realised when social equality, need fulfilment, full employment, equitable distribution and stability are achieved without heavy debt servicing, high inflation or ecological damage — and when families and society care for the vulnerable (p. 24)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Maqasid are a separate source of law that can override the Qur’an and Sunnah.","right":"In the book, the objectives reflect the spirit of Shari’ah and help jurists decide matters through Ijtihad and Qiyas; they were identified by inductive survey of the Qur’an and Sunnah."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.2.1","t5.5","t3.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is a PRIMARY objective of Shari’ah in the author’s list?","options":["Maintenance of peace and security","Protection of intellect","Promotion of social security","Cooperation in goodness"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Protection of intellect is primary; the others are secondary objectives (pp. 23–24)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.2.2","sectionTitle":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah","title":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: Primary and Secondary","covers":["t2.2.2"],"overview":"The author explains the higher objectives (Maqasid) that Shari'ah rules are designed to serve, distinguishing primary objectives (protection of religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour) from secondary ones (justice, social security, peace, cooperation, moral values).","simpleExplanation":"Shari'ah isn't just a list of rules — it exists to protect six fundamental things: religion, life, family, property, the mind, and honour/dignity. From these flow further goals like justice, helping the needy, peace, and promoting good over evil. The overall aim is human well-being and happiness, in this life and the next — not just material comfort.","academicExplanation":"Studying Maqasid matters because they reflect the spirit of Shari'ah and guide jurists' Ijtihad/Qiyas in determining permissibility. Catering to human well-being in this world and the Hereafter, or relieving hardship, is the basic objective; Islam takes a positive view of life (man as vicegerent/Khalifah of God), requiring a morally responsible life, fair earning, and treating wealth as a trust (Amanah) for which account is rendered to Allah. Unlike conventional economics (where livelihood/economic development is the ultimate goal), Islamic economics treats livelihood as necessary but not the sole purpose of life — the Hereafter is the real concern. Jurists (Ghazali, Shatbi, later Tahir ibn Ashoor) identified six Primary Objectives via inductive survey of Qur'an/Sunnah: protection/preservation of (1) Religion, (2) Life (including the law of Qisas and provision of basic necessities), (3) Progeny/family unit (marriage, chastity, upbringing), (4) Property (sanctity of Halal wealth, discouraging wealth concentration, governed by the law of Mu'amalat), (5) Intellect (acquiring knowledge), (6) Honour (prohibition of false accusation, right to privacy). These lead to five Secondary Objectives: establishing justice/equity, promoting social security/mutual help (especially for the poor), maintaining peace/security, promoting cooperation in good and prohibiting evil, and promoting universal moral values/preservation of nature. Chapra is quoted arguing well-being (in the Islamic sense) must include humanitarian/spiritual goals — social equality, need fulfilment, full employment, equitable distribution, economic stability without heavy debt-servicing burdens, plus family/social solidarity — and that material and spiritual well-being are interrelated, not independent. The author also lists eight basic socio-economic rights derived from Qur'an/Sunnah study: to safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, to satisfaction of basic needs, to redress, to education, and to a healthy environment; rulers/regulators must protect these against unscrupulous exploitation.","examExplanation":"Six Primary Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: protection of Religion, Life, Progeny, Property, Intellect, Honour. Five Secondary Objectives flowing from these: justice/equity, social security & mutual help, peace & security, promotion of good/prohibition of evil, and universal moral values. Chapra's expanded view of 'well-being' includes both material (need fulfilment, equitable distribution, stability without heavy debt burdens) and spiritual/humanitarian goals (family and social solidarity) — the two are interrelated, not separable. Eight derived socio-economic rights: safety, information, choice, being heard, basic-needs satisfaction, redress, education, healthy environment.","keyPoints":["Six Primary Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: protection/preservation of Religion, Life, Progeny (family), Property, Intellect, and Honour.","Five Secondary Objectives: establishing justice and equity; promoting social security, mutual help and solidarity; maintaining peace and security; promoting cooperation in goodness and prohibiting evil; promoting universal moral values and preservation of nature.","Islam distinguishes 'happiness' (Falah — its objective) from mere 'pleasure' (the objective of positive/secular economics).","Chapra argues true well-being must include humanitarian/spiritual goals (family and social solidarity) alongside material goals (need fulfilment, equitable distribution, stability without heavy debt-servicing burden), and that the two are interrelated.","Eight basic socio-economic rights derived from Qur'an/Sunnah: safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, satisfaction of basic needs, redress, education, and a healthy environment."],"definitions":[{"term":"Maqasid al-Shari'ah","definition":"The higher objectives/purposes that Shari'ah rulings are designed to serve, reflecting the spirit of Shari'ah and guiding jurists' reasoning (Ijtihad/Qiyas)."},{"term":"Falah","definition":"Welfare/success in this world and the Hereafter — the Islamic concept of true happiness/well-being, distinct from mere worldly pleasure."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Wealth is a trust (stewardship) from Allah for which man is accountable, not an end in itself for boasting, arrogance or oppression."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes list only the six Primary Objectives and forget the five Secondary Objectives that derive from them — exam questions often test both tiers."],"distinctions":["Falah (Islamic concept of happiness/well-being, spanning this world and the Hereafter) vs pleasure/utility maximization (the objective of conventional positive economics)."],"relatedConcepts":["Shari'ah","Mu'amalat","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,23,24,25]}}],"table":{"caption":"Maqasid al-Shari’ah as presented by the author","head":["Primary objectives (protection of)","Secondary objectives"],"rows":[["Religion","Justice and equity in society"],["Life","Social security, mutual help and solidarity"],["Progeny / family unit","Peace and security"],["Property","Cooperation in goodness; prohibition of evil"],["Intellect","Universal moral values; preservation of nature"],["Honour","—"]]}},{"id":"t2.3","section":"2.3","title":"Why Study Islamic Economics?","pages":[25,27],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["islamic-economics"],"intuition":"Is economics a “worldly” subject separate from religion? Islam rejects the split between the “pious” and the “worldly” person.","simpleExplanation":"Economics matters in Islam for two reasons: better sustenance and religious obligation. Islam links piety with caring for the needy, and its economic system focuses on care for the poor and justice.","academicExplanation":["Economic matters command a central place in Shari’ah. Man is concerned with material means of sustenance and religious beliefs, so studying economics serves both. Islam does not accept the “pious person” as distinct from the “worldly person”; verse 2:177 links righteousness with spending on kin, orphans, the needy and fulfilling contracts. Taqwa (piety) encompasses the love of fellow human beings.","M.A. Mannan gives seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics: ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical and international. Islamic economics cannot remain neutral between ends; it studies what is and what ought to be. Other imperatives: underdevelopment of the Muslim world, and the failure of secularist systems — trickle-down theories have failed, “hyper-consumption” coexists with deprivation, and the Enlightenment worldview weakened religion’s moral filtering. The Qur’an gives broad principles: an owner’s attitude to property, society’s attitude to the needy, cooperation, and a bias against concentration of wealth."],"examExplanation":"Dual purpose: sustenance + religious imperative; no pious/worldly divide (2:177). Mannan’s seven imperatives: ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international. Islamic economics is not neutral between ends.","keyPoints":["Taqwa relates piety to social realities.","Mannan’s seven imperatives.","Islamic economics studies both “what is” and “what ought to be”.","Main theme: care for the poor and socio-economic justice.","Mannan’s seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics: ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical and international (p. 26).","The Enlightenment’s secular worldview weakened the moral filter that religion provides (p. 26)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Piety and social concern (2:177)","kind":"textbook","text":"The verse defines righteousness to include spending on kin, orphans, the needy and travellers, and fulfilling contracts — so piety cannot be separated from economic conduct (pp. 25–26)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam separates the “pious person” from the “worldly person”.","right":"Islam integrates worship with economic, political and social life; verse 2:177 links righteousness to spending on kin, orphans, the needy and fulfilling contracts (p. 25)."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.3.1","t2.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is one of M.A. Mannan’s seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics?","options":["Technological","Historical","Agricultural","Monetary"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The seven are ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical and international (p. 26)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.3","sectionTitle":"Why Study Islamic Economics?","title":"Why Study Islamic Economics? The Integrated Islamic Worldview","covers":["t2.3","t2.3.1"],"overview":"The author argues economics deserves serious study within Islam because Islam does not separate 'worldly' from 'religious' life, and because the failure of secular economic systems to solve global poverty and injustice makes an alternative worth exploring.","simpleExplanation":"Islam doesn't separate spiritual life from economic life — earning a living and spending it wisely are themselves acts of devotion. Since conventional 'trickle-down' economics and the shrinking welfare state have failed to fix poverty and inequality, studying an economics grounded in justice and care for the poor is a strategic necessity, not just an academic exercise.","academicExplanation":"The author argues economic sustenance and religious belief are the two central concerns of human life, so Islam integrates devotion with guidance on economy, politics and international relations (Islam rejects a 'pious person' vs 'worldly person' dichotomy), citing Qur'an 2:177 on righteousness including both belief and spending on kin, orphans, the needy, travellers, etc., interpreted by Anis Ahmad as reflecting the Qur'an's social/human dimensions and its relevance to all humanity, not just Muslims. M.A. Mannan's seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics — ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international — are cited, along with his view that Islamic economics (unlike secular economics) cannot remain neutral between ends and must engage social, political, ethical and moral issues. Beyond intra-Muslim underdevelopment, the author cites the broader global failure of secularist systems: trickle-down theory and related theories of automatic poverty alleviation via growth have failed; the Western welfare state has given way to 'the least government is the best government'; rising 'hyper-consumption' among the rich (both developed and developing world) coexists with mass poverty and illiteracy. He also traces this partly to the Enlightenment's secularist worldview, which treated revealed religious truths as mere inventions, weakening religion's collective moral-sanctioning role. Since Islamic economics centers on care for the poor and socio-economic justice, studying it is framed as strategically necessary for economists and policymakers, grounded in the Qur'an's broad values (an owner's attitude to property, society's duty to the needy, cooperative economic relationships, and bias against wealth concentration).","examExplanation":"The author gives two main rationales for studying Islamic economics: (1) Islam integrates religious and economic/worldly life (no pious-vs-worldly dichotomy — cf. Qur'an 2:177); (2) the demonstrated failure of secular economic systems (trickle-down theory, shrinking welfare states, Enlightenment-driven secularism) to resolve poverty/inequality, alongside rising 'hyper-consumption' amid mass poverty, makes an alternative rooted in justice and care for the poor strategically relevant, not merely intra-Muslim.","keyPoints":["Islam does not separate the 'pious person' from the 'worldly person' — economic activity itself can be an act of devotion (Qur'an 2:177).","M.A. Mannan identifies seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics: ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international.","Islamic economics 'cannot remain neutral between different ends' — unlike secular economics, it is explicitly concerned with what ought to be, per Shari'ah.","Trickle-down theory and related growth-alone poverty-reduction theories are said to have failed; the Western welfare state has given way to minimal-government ideology.","The author links this partly to the Enlightenment's secularism, which weakened religion's role in providing collective moral sanction.","The main theme of Islamic economics — care for the poor and socio-economic justice — makes its study a strategic, not merely academic, priority."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Islamic economics is not value-neutral between ends; it is explicitly concerned with what 'ought to be' according to Shari'ah, unlike positive/secular economics."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["The author frames the case for studying Islamic economics as broader than 'helping Muslim countries develop' — it is presented as a response to a global failure of secularist economic systems, relevant to non-Muslims too."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Maqasid al-Shari'ah","Trickle-down theory","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[25,26,27]}}]},{"id":"t2.3.1","section":"2.3.1","title":"The Role of Islamic Economists","pages":[27,30],"tier":"detailed","concepts":["islamic-economics"],"intuition":"Did Muslim scholars contribute to economic thought, or is Islamic economics a recent invention?","simpleExplanation":"Early jurists analysed trade, prices, money and taxes. Ibn Khaldun anticipated ideas of Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes. Modern Islamic economists have covered many areas, but a complete model is still lacking.","academicExplanation":["Early jurists advised individuals and rulers and later analysed trading, prices, money, profit-sharing, taxes and development, focusing on justice, need fulfilment, efficiency, freedom and growth. Ibn Khaldun (1332–1406) supported free private enterprise, opposed government meddling through inequitable taxation or direct participation in production, anticipated Adam Smith (labour value; division of labour), Malthus (population) and Keynes (normative role of the State), and warned that absolute State power causes economic decline. Ibn Taymiyah discussed Thaman-e-mithl (market price/wage), pricing and the ombudsman; Nasiruddin Tusi advised on financial administration; Shah Waliullah on production and exchange.","Maurice Lombard’s The Golden Age of Islam is quoted on the Muslim world as the driving force of medieval trade, unified by the Dinar and Arabic. Modern pioneers (e.g. Mawdudi, Baqar Sadr) and later economists (e.g. Siddiqi, Chapra, Mirakhor, Mohsin Khan) have covered almost all areas, especially interest-free banking. However, these are segments not yet put together in a comprehensive model; implementation lacks uniformity, and because value judgements resist quantification, Islamic economics can for now be introduced in parts only."],"examExplanation":"Classical contributions (Ibn Khaldun: free enterprise, anti-State-monopoly, growth theory; Ibn Taymiyah: Thaman-e-mithl, Hisbah). Modern work is segmental (esp. interest-free banking); no comprehensive model yet; introduce in parts.","keyPoints":["Ibn Khaldun: considered by the author a founder of economic science.","Ibn Taymiyah: Thaman-e-mithl — normal/market price or wage.","Modern work is fragmented and lacks uniformity.","A complete welfare-function model may take longer.","Ibn Khaldun anticipated Adam Smith (labour value), Malthus (population) and Keynes (normative role of the State).","Ibn Taymiyah discussed Thaman-e-mithl (market price/wage) and the ombudsman.","Modern work is fragmented; no comprehensive model or welfare function yet (p. 29)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Thaman-e-mithl","definition":"Normal/market price or wage, discussed by Ibn Taymiyah."}],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Khaldun on state trading","kind":"textbook","text":"Ibn Khaldun held that a ruler’s revenue grows only through fair treatment of property owners; a ruler engaging in commerce and agriculture harms subjects and ruins revenue. He also saw productivity depending on market size and division of labour (pp. 27–28)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t2.3","t2.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the chapter, what is the main limitation of modern Islamic economics?","options":["It ignores banking","Its work consists of segments not yet put together into a comprehensive model","It rejects markets","It has no classical roots"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Work on segments exists, but not a comprehensive model (p. 29)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.3.1","sectionTitle":"The Role of Islamic Economists","title":"Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists","covers":["t2.3.1"],"overview":"The author surveys classical Muslim jurist-economists (especially Ibn Khaldun) and traces the modern revival of Islamic economics through named scholars, noting that this body of work remains fragmented rather than a unified model.","simpleExplanation":"Long before modern economics existed, Muslim scholars like Ibn Khaldun were already analyzing markets, taxation, labor and the State's economic role — Ibn Khaldun is credited with anticipating ideas later attributed to Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes. In modern times, many economists have revived and modernized this thinking, but their work remains scattered across separate topics rather than combined into one complete economic model.","academicExplanation":"Early Islamic jurists advised rulers on economic behaviour/policy and later analysed trading, prices, money, profit-sharing, taxes and development, emphasizing ethics, justice, need fulfilment, efficiency, freedom, growth and development. Named contributors include the Imams of the juristic schools (Abu Hanifa, Malik, Shafii, Ahmad ibn-e-Hanbal) and others (al-Sheibani, Abu Yusuf, al-Mawardi, Nizamul Mulk Tusi, Nasiruddin Tusi, Ghazali, Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn Qayyim, Ibn Khaldun, al-Maqrizi, Shah Waliullah). Ibn Khaldun (1332-1406) receives the most detailed treatment: his Muqaddamah (praised by Arnold Toynbee as unmatched in its kind) supported free private enterprise, opposed government interference in markets via inequitable taxation or direct production/distribution participation, and warned that State monopoly ruins the economy. He is credited with anticipating Adam Smith (labour value theory), Malthus (population theory) and Keynes (normative State role), plus a coherent theory of economic growth — making him arguably the founder of economic science; he also linked productivity to market extent, division of labour and specialization, and warned that the growth of absolute State power (funded by rising taxation/confiscation and expanding bureaucracy/army/police) causes economic decline. Ibn Taymiyah discussed Thaman-e-mithl (normal/market price or wage), economic freedom, market pricing, the ombudsman's role, and government's functions. Nasiruddin Tusi's 13th-century Memorandum guided Mongol-era Iranian financial administration; Shah Waliullah discussed production/exchange principles. Maurice Lombard's The Golden Age of Islam is quoted describing the Muslim world's medieval role as the 'driving force' of trade/culture (unified by the Dinar and Arabic as commercial language) while the West was a relative 'void', its later 11th-century decline into regional fragmentation, and its continued influence in science/medicine/philosophy even post-decline. The modern revival is traced through first-generation pioneers (Syed Qutab, Mawdudi, Baqar Sadre, Hameedullah, etc.) and subsequent economists (Anwar Iqbal Qureshi, Nejatullah Siddiqi, M.A. Mannan, M. Umer Chapra, Abbas Mirakhor, Mohsin S. Khan, and many others) who have addressed most areas of modern economics (market forces, production, distribution, money, self-interest, welfare economics, government finance). However, the author stresses this remains segmented work — not yet integrated into one comprehensive model — with inconsistent implementation across countries, partly because value-judgement-laden areas resist quantification/uniformity.","examExplanation":"Ibn Khaldun (1332-1406), author of the Muqaddamah, is the standout classical figure: he supported free private enterprise, opposed State market interference and monopoly, and is credited with anticipating Adam Smith (labour value), Malthus (population) and Keynes (State's normative role) — arguably making him the founder of economic science. Ibn Taymiyah contributed concepts like Thaman-e-mithl (fair/market price). The modern revival (20th century onward) produced extensive segmental work (Siddiqi, Chapra, Mannan, Mirakhor, Mohsin Khan, etc.) across banking, Zakat, fiscal policy and regulation, but the author stresses this has not yet been unified into one comprehensive Islamic economic model, and implementation across countries lacks uniformity.","keyPoints":["Ibn Khaldun's Muqaddamah supported free private enterprise and opposed State interference/monopoly in markets, warning that State monopoly ruins the economy.","Ibn Khaldun is credited with anticipating Adam Smith (labour value theory), Malthus (population theory) and Keynes (normative State role), and presenting a coherent growth theory.","Ibn Khaldun warned that growth of absolute State power (via expanding bureaucracy/army/taxation) causes economic decline.","Ibn Taymiyah developed the concept of Thaman-e-mithl (normal/market price or wage) and discussed market pricing, the ombudsman's role and government functions.","Maurice Lombard's account credits the medieval Muslim world as the 'driving force' of trade and culture, unified by the Dinar and Arabic, before fragmenting after crises in the late 11th century.","Modern Islamic economics (led by figures like Siddiqi, Chapra, Mannan, Mirakhor, Mohsin Khan) has addressed most areas of modern economics but remains a set of segments, not yet a unified comprehensive model, with inconsistent cross-country implementation."],"definitions":[{"term":"Thaman-e-mithl","definition":"Normal/market price that has to be paid in case any issue arises in deciding the actual price in a transaction (concept developed by Ibn Taymiyah)."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Ibn Khaldun: State monopoly in commerce/agriculture and excessive government interference in the market mechanism ruin the economy."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Khaldun on the ruler's finances","kind":"textbook","text":"Ibn Khaldun argued a ruler's finances improve only through equitable tax treatment of people with property, warning that a ruler engaging directly in commerce/agriculture becomes ruinous to revenue and cultural activity."}],"commonConfusions":["Ibn Khaldun's ideas 'anticipating' Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes is the author's/cited scholars' claim about intellectual priority, not a claim that Ibn Khaldun originated modern capitalist theory wholesale."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Muqaddamah","Thaman-e-mithl","Al-Hisbah"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,28,29,30]}}]},{"id":"t2.4","section":"2.4","title":"Islamic Economics: What Should it be?","pages":[30,31],"tier":"core","concepts":["islamic-economics","hisbah"],"intuition":"Is Islamic economics a value-free science or a value-laden system? It is normative — it asks what should be.","simpleExplanation":"Positive economics describes; normative economics prescribes. Islamic economics is normative: wealth is a means to meet needs, and people must consider the impact of their actions on others. It aims at equitable (not equal) distribution and relies on the market, with the ombudsman (Hisbah) checking abuses.","academicExplanation":["Economics proper (positive economics) scientifically explains behaviour under scarcity; it is value neutral — it describes but does not prescribe (e.g. Lionel Robbins’ definition). Normative or welfare economics (“political economy”) involves value judgements. In the Islamic approach, development and abundant wealth are means to satisfy human needs; verse 28:77 links the Hereafter with one’s worldly share. People must follow Shari’ah dos and don’ts and consider the impact of their activities on society. The State should filter wants, motivate people to abstain from injurious activities, and restructure the system to transfer resources towards the dual objective.","Islamic economics mainly seeks to remove injustice and inequality. It accepts the concomitants of a market economy — right of ownership, freedom of enterprise, competition — but aims at equitable, not equal, distribution (like blood distributed to organs according to their function). Because Shari’ah is noncoercive, the market is relied upon; the Prophet (pbuh) discouraged price fixing when fluctuations were due to market forces, but where monopolistic or unjust practices existed, Al-Hisbah (the ombudsman) was empowered to check imbalances."],"examExplanation":"Positive (describes, value-neutral) vs normative (prescribes, value judgements). Islamic economics is normative: wealth as means; equitable not equal distribution; accepts ownership, enterprise, competition; no price fixing under genuine market forces; Hisbah checks monopoly/unjust practices.","keyPoints":["Positive economics describes; normative prescribes.","Equitable, not equal, distribution.","Price fixing discouraged when prices move due to market forces alone.","Hisbah (ombudsman) checks monopolistic and unjust practices."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hisbah (Al-Hisbah)","definition":"Institution of the ombudsman empowered as a social regulatory body to check monopolistic and unjust pricing, production and distribution practices."},{"term":"Positive economics","definition":"Economics proper — value-neutral scientific explanation of behaviour under scarcity; describes rather than prescribes."}],"examples":[{"title":"Equitable, not equal","kind":"textbook","text":"Islamic economics does not aim to give everyone the same income but a fair share — like blood, which is distributed to each organ according to its function. Price fixing is discouraged when prices move only because of market forces; the Hisbah checks monopolies and abuse (pp. 30–31)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam requires the State to fix prices.","right":"The Prophet (pbuh) discouraged price fixing when fluctuations were due to market forces alone; intervention via Hisbah is for monopolistic or unjust practices."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.4.1","t2.5.5","t3.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Islamic economics aims at what kind of distribution of income and wealth?","options":["Equal","Equitable","Market-determined without limits","State-owned"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Equitable, not equal, distribution (p. 30)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.4","sectionTitle":"Islamic Economics: What should it be?","title":"Positive versus Normative Economics in the Islamic Framework","covers":["t2.4","t2.4.1"],"overview":"The author distinguishes positive economics (value-neutral, descriptive) from normative/welfare economics (value-laden, prescriptive), and situates Islamic economics as normative but grounded in market mechanisms, moderated by ethics and limited State intervention (Al-Hisbah).","simpleExplanation":"Positive economics just describes how people actually behave, without judging right or wrong. Normative economics makes recommendations based on values. Islamic economics is normative — it wants outcomes shaped by justice and welfare — but it still uses markets and generally lets prices move freely, stepping in (through an ombudsman-like institution called Hisbah) only when monopolistic or unjust practices distort the market.","academicExplanation":"Positive economics (per Lionel Robbins's definition) is value-neutral, dealing scientifically with behaviour under scarcity — describing, not prescribing. Normative/welfare economics ('political economy') necessarily involves value judgements. The Islamic approach treats wealth creation as a means to satisfy human needs and support society, not for boasting or oppression (Qur'an 28:77 on seeking the Hereafter without neglecting one's worldly portion), requiring believers to weigh their economic activities' impact on others, with the State controlling harmful wants via a 'filtering process' and restructuring resource transfers to realize dual (worldly/Hereafter) objectives. Unlike conventional welfare economics' utilitarian 'greatest satisfaction of the greatest number', Islamic economics aims chiefly at removing injustice/inequality while still accepting core market-economy concomitants (ownership rights, freedom of enterprise, competition) — differing from present market systems in vision. Shari'ah 'indicates directions' toward justice/well-being/security/knowledge without coercively imposing them, aiming for equitable (not equal) distribution — likened to blood distributed unequally among body organs according to need/function. Because Shari'ah is noncoercive, the market is treated as a natural mechanism of ethical transformation: the Prophet (pbuh) discouraged price-fixing intervention during ordinary market-driven price fluctuations, but where undue monopolistic/unjust pricing or distribution existed, Al-Hisbah (the institution of the ombudsman) was empowered as a social regulatory body to restore just market functioning.","examExplanation":"Positive economics = value-neutral, descriptive (per Lionel Robbins). Normative/welfare economics = value-laden, prescriptive. Islamic economics is normative, aiming to remove injustice/inequality while retaining core market features (ownership, enterprise freedom, competition). It seeks equitable (not equal) distribution, and relies on the market as the default mechanism (the Prophet discouraged price-fixing during normal fluctuations), intervening via Al-Hisbah (ombudsman institution) specifically against monopolistic/unjust practices.","keyPoints":["Positive economics (per Lionel Robbins) is value-neutral and descriptive; normative/welfare economics involves value judgements and is prescriptive.","Islamic economics is normative, aiming chiefly to remove injustice and inequality, while still accepting market-economy features: ownership rights, freedom of enterprise, competition.","The goal is equitable (not equal) distribution of income and wealth — likened to blood distributed unequally among body organs according to their differing functions.","Because Shari'ah is noncoercive in economic matters, the market is relied upon as the default, natural mechanism; the Prophet (pbuh) discouraged price-fixing during ordinary market-driven fluctuations.","Al-Hisbah (the institution of the ombudsman) is empowered specifically to check undue monopolistic and unjust pricing/production/distribution practices."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al-Hisbah","definition":"The institution of the ombudsman — a social regulatory body empowered to check market imbalances (monopolistic/unjust pricing, production or distribution practices) and restore just, market-driven exchange."}],"conditions":["Price-fixing intervention is discouraged where price fluctuations arise from genuine market forces alone; intervention (via Al-Hisbah) is warranted only against undue monopolistic and unjust practices."],"principles":["Islamic economics targets equitable, not equal, distribution of income and wealth — analogous to blood unequally but appropriately distributed among the body's organs."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Islamic economics accepting 'the market' does not mean laissez-faire without limits — it means market pricing is the default mechanism, subject to intervention against genuine market distortion (monopoly, unjust practices), not routine price control."],"distinctions":["Equitable distribution (fairness given differing roles/needs, the Islamic economics goal) vs equal distribution (identical shares regardless of role/need, not the goal)."],"relatedConcepts":["Al-Hisbah","Positive economics","Normative economics","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Positive economics","b":"Normative (welfare) economics","text":"Positive describes what is, value-neutral; normative involves value judgements about what should be."},{"a":"Equal distribution","b":"Equitable distribution","text":"Islam seeks equitable distribution — like blood distributed to organs according to function — not equal shares."}]},{"id":"t2.4.1","section":"2.4.1","title":"Islamic Economics Defined","pages":[31,32],"tier":"core","concepts":["islamic-economics"],"intuition":"How would you define Islamic economics in an exam? The book gives several scholarly definitions.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic economics studies economic problems in the light of Islamic values: how to create, own, distribute and spend wealth for the welfare of people within Shari’ah. Production techniques are universal; the distribution system is what differs.","academicExplanation":["Definitions quoted: Ibn Khaldun — the desire for food and requirements and efforts to obtain them; a science of managing households and cities according to reason and ethics. Mohsin S. Khan — a complete system prescribing a pattern of social and economic behaviour, covering property rights, incentives, allocation, economic freedom, decision-making and the role of government, with social justice as the overriding objective. S.M. Hasanuz Zaman — “the knowledge of application of injunctions and rules of the Shari’ah that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources in order to provide satisfaction to individuals and enable them to perform their obligations to Allah and society.”","Islamic economics is thus a social science studying economic problems in the light of Islamic values. Economic science (means of production) is universal, but the economic system (how wealth is distributed, possessed, spent) depends on ideology — the difference from capitalist and socialist systems. The integrated Islamic social framework rests on: equilibrium between work and worship; human equality; mutual responsibilities; distributive justice; balanced use of the bounties of God; limited sovereignty of self-interest; co-existence; freedom of conscience."],"examExplanation":"Hasanuz Zaman: knowledge of application of Shari’ah rules that stop injustice in acquisition and disposition of material resources, to satisfy individuals and enable them to fulfil obligations to Allah and society. Science universal; system ideology-based. Eight criteria of the integrated framework.","keyPoints":["Three definitions: Ibn Khaldun, Mohsin S. Khan, S.M. Hasanuz Zaman.","Difference with capitalism/socialism lies in distribution, ownership and spending.","Eight criteria of the integrated Islamic social framework.","Eight criteria of the Islamic social framework: work–worship balance, human equality, mutual responsibility, distributive justice, balanced use of God’s bounties, limited individual sovereignty, co-existence and freedom of conscience (p. 32)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Islamic economics (Hasanuz Zaman)","definition":"Knowledge of application of injunctions and rules of the Shari’ah that stop injustice in acquisition and disposition of material resources, to provide satisfaction to individuals and enable them to perform obligations to Allah and society."}],"examples":[{"title":"Same machine, different system","kind":"practice","text":"Two factories use identical technology. What differs under Islamic economics is how the resulting wealth is owned, distributed and spent — the “economic system”, not the “economic science” of production (pp. 31–32)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t1.7","t2.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Whose definition describes Islamic economics as knowledge of applying Shari’ah rules that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources?","options":["Ibn Khaldun","Mohsin S. Khan","S.M. Hasanuz Zaman","Lionel Robbins"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"This is S.M. Hasanuz Zaman’s definition (p. 31)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.4.1","sectionTitle":"Islamic Economics Defined","title":"Defining Islamic Economics: Scholarly Definitions and the Integrated Model","covers":["t2.4.1"],"overview":"The author surveys several scholars' definitions of Islamic economics and lists eight criteria of the integrated Islamic social framework, positioning Islamic economics as a value-laden social science distinct from capitalism and socialism specifically in its rules on ownership and distribution.","simpleExplanation":"Different scholars define Islamic economics slightly differently, but the common thread is: it's the study of how to create, own, use and distribute wealth fairly, guided by Islamic values, with the ultimate goal of social justice — not just efficient production. What makes it different from capitalism or socialism isn't the technical process of producing goods (that's universal) but the rules on ownership and distribution of wealth.","academicExplanation":"The author surveys definitions: Ibn Khaldun saw economics as the pursuit of food/necessities and the science of managing households/cities per reason and ethics. Mohsin S. Khan (IMF) defines Islamic economics as a complete system prescribing social/economic behaviour, covering property rights, incentives, resource allocation, economic freedom, decision-making and government's role, with social justice and specific income/wealth distribution patterns as the overriding objective. S.M. Hasanuz Zaman (IDB Laureate) defines it as the knowledge of applying Shari'ah injunctions that stop injustice in acquiring/disposing of material resources, to satisfy individuals and enable their obligations to Allah and society. The author synthesizes these as: Islamic economics is a social science studying economic problems in light of Islamic values, i.e. resource use for people's welfare within the Shari'ah framework — once that framework is adopted, it determines production, trade, finance and distribution. He notes the means of production are universal (economic science is the same everywhere), but the economic system determining distribution/ownership/disposal differs by ideology — this is precisely where Islamic economics differs from capitalism/socialism. The 'integrated model' of the Islamic social framework rests on eight criteria: equilibrium between work and worship; human equality; mutual responsibilities in society; distributive justice; balanced/beneficent use of God's bounties; limited sovereignty of individual self-interest for the benefit of others/society; the principle of co-existence; and freedom of conscience.","examExplanation":"Key definitions: Mohsin S. Khan (IMF) — Islamic economics is a complete system with social justice as its overriding objective, covering property rights, incentives, resource allocation and government's role. Hasanuz Zaman — the knowledge of applying Shari'ah to stop injustice in acquiring/disposing of resources. The author's synthesis: resource use for people's welfare within the Shari'ah framework. Islamic economics differs from capitalism/socialism specifically in its rules on ownership and distribution (not in the universal technical process of production). Eight criteria of the integrated Islamic social framework: work-worship equilibrium, human equality, mutual responsibility, distributive justice, balanced use of God's bounties, limited individual sovereignty, co-existence, freedom of conscience.","keyPoints":["Mohsin S. Khan (IMF): Islamic economics prescribes a complete system of social/economic behaviour with social justice as the overriding objective.","S.M. Hasanuz Zaman: Islamic economics is the application of Shari'ah injunctions to stop injustice in acquiring/disposing of material resources.","The means of production are universal across economic systems; what differs by ideology is how wealth is owned, distributed and disposed of — this is precisely where Islamic economics diverges from capitalism and socialism.","Eight criteria of the integrated Islamic social framework: equilibrium between work and worship; human equality; mutual responsibilities in society; distributive justice; balanced/beneficent use of God's bounties; limited sovereignty of individual self-interest; the principle of co-existence; freedom of conscience."],"definitions":[{"term":"Islamic economics (Mohsin S. Khan)","definition":"A complete system prescribing a specific pattern of social and economic behaviour, covering property rights, incentives, resource allocation, economic freedom, economic decision-making and the government's role, with social justice and specific patterns of income/wealth distribution as the overriding objective."},{"term":"Islamic economics (Hasanuz Zaman)","definition":"The knowledge of application of injunctions and rules of Shari'ah that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources, to satisfy individuals and enable them to perform their obligations to Allah and society."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Islamic economics is not distinguished from capitalism/socialism by different production techniques (these are universal) — the distinction lies in ownership and distribution rules, per the author's framework."],"distinctions":["Economic science (universal, deals with production) vs economic system (ideology-specific, deals with ownership, disposal, distribution) — this recurring distinction underlies the definitions in this section."],"relatedConcepts":["Distributive justice","Economic system"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[31,32]}}]},{"id":"t2.5","section":"2.5","title":"Paraphernalia of Islamic Economics","pages":[32,33],"tier":"core","concepts":["islamic-economics","riba","zakat"],"intuition":"What would an ideal Islamic macro-economy look like? The author names its key features.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic economics seeks efficiency and equity through markets, but with State facilitation and oversight. Its key macro features: abolition of interest, promotion of trade and real business, profit-sharing, Zakat, avoidance of wasteful consumption and an effective overseeing State.","academicExplanation":["Like any system, it aims at efficiency and equity in allocation and distribution, recognising markets and freedom but also the adverse impact of an unregulated market. Adam Smith’s “invisible hand” has not fulfilled social obligations; hence Islamic economics allows ample room for State intervention to achieve an optimal mix of self-interested market players and social interest.","The system comprises: ownership of commodities and wealth; transfer of ownership; distribution of wealth. Key macroeconomic features of an ideal Islamic economy: abolition of interest (Riba); promotion of trading and real business; profit-sharing as a tool; application of Zakat; avoidance of wasteful consumption (Israf); and an effective overseeing role of the State."],"examExplanation":"Key macro features: abolish Riba; promote trade/real business; profit-sharing; Zakat; avoid Israf; effective State oversight. Three elements: ownership, transfer, distribution.","keyPoints":["Markets plus State facilitation and intervention.","Six key macroeconomic features.","Goals: need fulfilment, stable growth, equitable distribution, ecological coherence.","Key macro features of an ideal Islamic economy: abolition of Riba, promotion of real trade, profit-sharing, Zakat, avoidance of Israf and an effective overseeing State (p. 33)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Israf","definition":"Wasteful consumption, to be avoided in an Islamic economy."}],"examples":[{"title":"Checklist of an Islamic economy","kind":"practice","text":"Test an economy against the six macro features: is Riba abolished, is real trade promoted, is profit-sharing used, is Zakat applied, is wasteful consumption (Israf) avoided, and does the State oversee effectively? (p. 33)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic economics relies on the “invisible hand” like laissez-faire.","right":"It recognises markets but also their adverse impact on the poor; it combines self-interest with State facilitation and oversight (p. 32)."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.5.1","t2.5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is NOT listed among the key macroeconomic features of an ideal Islamic economy?","options":["Abolition of interest","Profit-sharing as a tool","Application of Zakat","Fixed returns on deposits"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Fixed returns on deposits are not listed; the features are abolition of Riba, trade, profit-sharing, Zakat, avoiding Israf and State oversight (p. 33)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.5","sectionTitle":"Paraphernalia of Islamic Economics / Ownership of Resources and Property Rights","title":"Ownership of Resources and Property Rights in Islamic Economics","covers":["t2.5","t2.5.1","t2.5.2","t2.5.3","t2.5.4","t2.5.5"],"overview":"The author explains that ultimate ownership belongs to Allah, with humans holding wealth in trust; Islam sets limits on the quality/means of acquiring wealth (not on its quantity), and lists the recognized means of legitimately possessing property.","simpleExplanation":"In Islamic economics, everything ultimately belongs to God, and people are trustees who may earn and use wealth according to His rules. There's no cap on how much wealth a person can have — the limits are on how it's acquired (it must be earned lawfully), not on the amount. Wealth can be acquired through work, inheritance, purchase, gifts, or state grants.","academicExplanation":"Islamic economics rests on the belief that all resources belong to the Creator; human beings hold them in trust as His vicegerents (khulafa), free to earn/spend per His commands, evidenced by Qur'an 24:33 ('give them from the Mal of Allah, which He gave to you') and 57:7 ('spend from what He put you in charge of'). Ownership by man is thus a divinely permitted right to utilize goods/assets, not absolute ownership. Islam sets limits on acquisition in terms of quality/means (not quantity) — ensuring wealth remains attainable to all despite differing abilities, while quality limits prevent human greed from corrupting the economy; this also respects humans' natural drive to work diligently and enjoy comforts. Recognized means of possessing goods: work, inheritance, purchasing/obtaining property for sustenance, properties received as gifts, and the State granting possession to citizens. Legal ownership and its transfer occur through a variety of Shari'ah-defined contracts (sale, loan, lease, gift), with room for resolving new issues via Ijtihad within Shari'ah limits. Alongside property rights, income/profit entitlement is established through effort, work, or taking responsibility (Dhaman), and distributed via partnership, trade, joint ventures, loans, grants and Zakat, plus control of wasteful spending — linking market-level productive activity with institutional-level policy and control.","examExplanation":"Ultimate ownership of all resources belongs to Allah; humans hold wealth as trustees/vicegerents (Qur'an 24:33, 57:7). Islam limits acquisition by quality/means (lawful, ethical methods), not by quantity — there is no wealth cap, but the method of earning must be Halal. Recognized means of acquiring property: work, inheritance, purchase/exchange, gifts, and State grants. Income/profit entitlement is grounded in effort, work, or taking liability (Dhaman) — not mere capital ownership.","keyPoints":["All resources ultimately belong to Allah; human ownership is a trust (stewardship/vicegerency), not absolute ownership.","Islam limits wealth acquisition in terms of quality/means (lawful methods), not quantity — there is no ceiling on how much wealth one may lawfully hold.","Recognized means of legitimately acquiring property: work, inheritance, purchasing/obtaining for sustenance, gifts, and State grants of possession.","Income/profit entitlement in Islamic economics is grounded in effort, work, or bearing responsibility (Dhaman) — this recurring theme underlies why capital alone (without risk/effort) cannot claim a guaranteed return."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mal (Property)","definition":"Anything that can be possessed: money (gold, silver), commodities (clothes, foodstuffs), and immovable property (houses, factories), and all other things which are possessed."},{"term":"Dhaman","definition":"Taking liability or responsibility — e.g. a contract of guarantee, or the responsibility borne by an entrepreneur/manager of a business."}],"conditions":["Wealth acquisition is limited by quality/means (must be through lawful — Halal — methods), not by quantity."],"principles":["Human ownership of wealth is a divinely permitted trust (vicegerency), not absolute, unconditional ownership."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Islamic economics does not cap how much wealth an individual may hold — the constraint is on the lawfulness of the method of acquisition, not the amount accumulated."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Zakat","Mal","Dhaman","Halal"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[32,33,34]}}]},{"id":"t2.5.1","section":"2.5.1","title":"Ownership of Resources and Property Rights","pages":[33,34],"tier":"core","concepts":["ownership","daman"],"intuition":"If everything belongs to God, can people own property? Yes — ownership is a trust and divine permission to use assets, with limits on how, not how much, one acquires.","simpleExplanation":"People hold resources in trust. They may earn and own wealth by lawful means (work, inheritance, purchase, gifts, State grants). Limits concern the quality of means, not the quantity of wealth. Income and profit come from effort, work or taking responsibility (Dhaman).","academicExplanation":["All resources belong to the Creator; humans hold them in trust as vicegerents. Individuals may earn and enjoy wealth acquired by legal means and through inheritance. Ownership is divine permission to use goods and assets (24:33; 57:7). Limits relate to the quality/means of acquiring, not the quantity of wealth, since quantity limits would resist diligent work while quality limits prevent greed from corrupting the economy.","Means of possessing goods: work, inheritance, purchase for sustenance, gifts and State grants. Islam provides legal means of ownership and transfer through contracts (sale, loan, lease, gift) with general rules and scope for Ijtihad. Income and profit entitlement arise through effort, work or taking responsibility (Dhaman), with distribution through partnership, trade, joint ventures, loans, transfer incomes (grants, Zakat) and control of waste."],"examExplanation":"Resources held in trust; ownership = divine permission to use. Limits on means (quality), not quantity. Means of possession: work, inheritance, purchase, gift, State grant. Profit entitlement via effort/work/responsibility (Dhaman).","keyPoints":["Ownership as trust (vicegerency).","Limits on the quality of means, not quantity of wealth.","Five means of possession.","Dhaman (responsibility/liability) is a basis for profit."],"definitions":[{"term":"Dhaman","definition":"Taking responsibility/liability — along with effort and work, a basis of entitlement to income and profit."}],"examples":[{"title":"Means of acquiring property","kind":"textbook","text":"Goods may be possessed through work, inheritance, purchase, gifts and grants by the State; limits concern the quality (means) of acquisition, not the quantity (pp. 33–34)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam caps how much wealth a person may own.","right":"The limits are in terms of the quality or means of acquiring wealth, not its quantity."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.2","t4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Islamic limits on acquiring wealth are expressed in terms of…","options":["Quantity of wealth","Quality/means of acquiring","Income tax brackets","Age of the owner"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Limits are in terms of the quality or means of acquiring, not quantity (p. 33)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t2.5.2","section":"2.5.2","title":"Islamic Welfare Approach","pages":[34,35],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["falah","distributive-justice"],"intuition":"What does “welfare” mean if it includes the Hereafter? It combines material and spiritual well-being, starting with everyone’s basic needs.","simpleExplanation":"Islam aims to meet everyone’s basic needs — food, clothing and housing — by requiring capable people to work, relatives and neighbours to help, and finally the State to support those who cannot. Luxury and conspicuous consumption are discouraged; the goal is Falah.","academicExplanation":["Welfare in Islam is neither exclusively materialistic nor absolutely spiritual. Allocation must fulfil the basic needs (food, clothing, housing) of all irrespective of colour, race or creed. Each capable person is obliged to work; the State must help those willing to work find employment. Self-interest alone conflicts with the core value of moderation; luxury and conspicuous consumption are not encouraged.","If some cannot earn, relatives and neighbours must support them, and failing that the State is responsible (“The Imam is in charge and responsible for his citizens”). Economists agree basic-needs fulfilment is the most important objective of distributive policy, though they differ on which needs to guarantee and how. Maximisation of Falah (welfare in this world and the Hereafter) is central; its elements are virtually impossible to quantify — the greatest challenge for Islamic economists (Chapra notes little progress on microeconomics)."],"examExplanation":"Welfare = material + spiritual. Basic needs (food, clothing, housing) for all: work obligation → family/neighbours → State. Moderation; no conspicuous consumption. Goal: Falah; hard to quantify.","keyPoints":["Order of responsibility for need fulfilment: self → relatives/neighbours → State.","Moderation over luxury.","Falah is hard to quantify — a challenge for model-building."],"definitions":[{"term":"Falah","definition":"Welfare in this world and the Hereafter."}],"examples":[{"title":"Order of responsibility for need","kind":"textbook","text":"A healthy adult must first work to meet his needs; if he cannot, relatives and neighbours must help; failing them, the State is responsible — “The Imam is in charge and responsible for his citizens” (pp. 34–35)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam guarantees luxury consumption as part of welfare.","right":"Moderation means necessities plus some comforts; luxury and conspicuous consumption are not encouraged (p. 34)."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.2.2","t2.5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If an individual cannot earn and has no relatives able to help, who is responsible for their basic needs?","options":["Nobody","The State","Banks","Foreign donors"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Islam obliges the State to support such citizens (p. 35)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.5.2","sectionTitle":"Islamic Welfare Approach","title":"The Islamic Welfare Approach: Need Fulfilment and Falah","covers":["t2.5.2"],"overview":"The author describes how Islamic economics balances material and spiritual welfare, obliges individuals to work and earn, and establishes a cascading responsibility (individual, then relatives/neighbours, then the State) to ensure everyone's basic needs are met.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic welfare isn't purely material or purely spiritual — both matter together. Everyone capable of working is expected to earn their own living and meet their basic needs (food, clothing, housing) without excess or luxury. If someone can't provide for themselves, first their family/neighbours are obligated to help; if no one can, the State must step in — the ruler is described as directly responsible for the people.","academicExplanation":"The Islamic concept of welfare dovetails spiritual and material aspects as mutually reinforcing sources of true welfare and happiness. Basic principles from the Qur'an and Sunnah promote human development, enforce justice, stop exploitation, and aim at a contented, satisfied ('welfare') society through need fulfilment for all regardless of colour, race or creed — a matter the author treats as crucial given its centrality to socio-economic justice. Every capable individual is obliged to work to meet their own and their dependents' basic needs; the State is responsible for enabling willing workers to find employment. Islam's core value of 'moderation' (necessities plus some comforts, minimizing hardship) directly conflicts with self-interest-driven consumption; luxury and conspicuous consumption are discouraged. Where an individual cannot earn/meet their needs, Shari'ah obliges relationally-proximate others (neighbours, relatives) to support them; failing that, the State must support all citizens, especially the disabled and destitute, per the Prophet's statement that 'the Imam (ruler) is in-charge (Ra'iee) and responsible for his citizens.' While there is consensus among Islamic economists that need fulfilment is the most important distributive-policy objective, there is some disagreement on which needs are guaranteed and how. The ultimate aim is maximizing Falah (welfare in this world and the Hereafter), achieved through obedience to Allah in both Ibadat (worship) and Mu'amalat (economic activity/transactions) — as long as seeking Allah's pleasure is the final goal, worldly and spiritual objectives align. Chapra is cited noting the absence of a rigorous theoretical macroeconomic model showing how Islamic values/institutions interact across economy, society and polity to realize this vision, and that microeconomics is a particularly underdeveloped area — the quantification challenge is the field's greatest ongoing difficulty.","examExplanation":"Islamic welfare integrates material and spiritual well-being (not purely one or the other). Every capable person must work to meet their own/dependents' basic needs. If unable, the support obligation cascades: first relatives/neighbours, then the State (per the Hadith: the ruler is responsible for his citizens). The goal is Falah (welfare in this world and Hereafter), realized through obedience in both worship (Ibadat) and economic activity (Mu'amalat). Chapra notes Islamic economics still lacks a rigorous macro/microeconomic model showing exactly how these values translate into measurable outcomes — a key ongoing challenge.","keyPoints":["Islamic welfare dovetails material and spiritual aspects as mutually reinforcing, not as competing or separate goals.","Every capable individual is obliged to work to meet their own and dependents' basic needs (food, clothing, housing); the State must help enable employment.","The Islamic value of 'moderation' (necessities plus some comforts) conflicts with self-interest-driven luxury/conspicuous consumption, which is discouraged.","Support obligation cascades: individual self-support first, then relatives/neighbours, then the State (per the Hadith: 'The Imam (ruler) is in-charge and responsible for his citizens').","The ultimate objective is maximizing Falah (welfare in this world and the Hereafter) through obedience in both worship (Ibadat) and economic dealings (Mu'amalat).","Chapra notes Islamic economics still lacks a rigorous macro/microeconomic model demonstrating exactly how its values translate into measurable outcomes — a major ongoing challenge for the field."],"definitions":[{"term":"Falah","definition":"Welfare in this world and the Hereafter; success and well-being in both material and spiritual senses."},{"term":"Ibadat / Mu'amalat","definition":"Ibadat refers to acts of worship; Mu'amalat refers to transactions/dealings among people, including economic activity — both are avenues to seek Allah's pleasure and thus contribute to Falah."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Support for those unable to meet their own basic needs cascades from individual, to relatives/neighbours, to the State as the ultimate guarantor."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Need fulfilment is described as having broad consensus as the top distributive-policy objective among Islamic economists, but there is genuine disagreement on which specific needs are 'guaranteed' and by what mechanism — this is not a settled, uniform list."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Falah","Zakat","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]}}]},{"id":"t2.5.3","section":"2.5.3","title":"The Factors of Production","pages":[35,37],"tier":"core","concepts":["factors-of-production","risk-sharing","riba"],"intuition":"In conventional economics capital earns interest. What does capital earn in Islamic economics — and when?","simpleExplanation":"Capitalism rewards capital with interest, land with rent, labour with wages and the entrepreneur with profit. In Islamic economics money capital earns profit only as a residual and must bear any loss; rent is for assets whose form stays intact; wages for labour. Money itself is not capital that can earn a return by itself.","academicExplanation":["Land, labour and capital jointly create value and should share the produce; the distinctive Islamic feature is that capital bears the loss, if any, and a portion of wealth is retained as Zakat for those unable to contribute.","Capitalism: capital → interest; land (natural resources) → rent; labour → wages; entrepreneur → profit. Islamic economics: capital (means of production wholly consumed or altered in form in production) cannot fetch rent; its compensation is profit — the residual after paying all other parties, with loss borne as a shortfall in principal. Land (means whose corpus and form remain unaltered — land, machinery, houses, vehicles) → rent; labour (physical or mental, including organisation) → wages.","Profit is the result of the productivity of capital invested, or a reward for workmanship or shouldering responsibility — not a reward for capital or enterprise per se. If money capital is borrowed, the entrepreneur repays exactly the principal irrespective of profit or loss. If capital is provided for profit and the business loses, the capital reduces pro rata. The discussion suggests five factors: land, capital, labour, management and responsibility/liability. Money itself is not capital and cannot earn profit or rent in itself; the fund provider who bears loss is also an entrepreneur."],"examExplanation":"Capitalism: capital–interest, land–rent, labour–wages, entrepreneur–profit. Islam: capital–profit (residual, bears loss), land/non-consumables–rent, labour–wages; profit from productivity, workmanship or responsibility. Five factors: land, capital, labour, management, responsibility. Loaned money returns principal only.","keyPoints":["Capital bears the loss, if any.","Rent only for assets whose corpus remains intact.","Profit is a residual after wages, rent and expenses.","Loan capital: repay principal only, whatever the outcome.","Five factors: land, capital, labour, management, responsibility/liability."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Entrepreneur using own and others’ capital","kind":"textbook","text":"An entrepreneur hiring land, labour and machinery pays wages and rent as agreed; profit arises only if there is a residual. If capital was borrowed, exactly the principal is repaid; if it was provided for profit and the business loses, the provider’s capital reduces pro rata."},{"title":"Entrepreneur with borrowed vs invested money","kind":"textbook","text":"If money is borrowed, the entrepreneur repays the same amount whatever the outcome; if it is provided for profit, a loss reduces that capital pro rata and the provider bears it (p. 36)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Money is a factor of production that can earn a fixed return.","right":"Money itself is not recognised as capital that earns profit in itself; it cannot claim rent because it is consumed when used."},{"wrong":"An investor is entitled to profit simply for owning capital.","right":"Profit is a residual earned through productivity, workmanship or responsibility; the capital provider must accept any loss."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2","t12.3","t12.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Islamic economics, which reward is appropriate for a machine (whose corpus remains intact) provided for production?","options":["Interest","Rent","Zakat","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Assets whose corpus and form remain unaltered can be leased for rent (p. 36)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.5.3","sectionTitle":"The Factors of Production","title":"Factors of Production: Islamic Economics versus Capitalism","covers":["t2.5.3"],"overview":"The author contrasts capitalism's four factors of production (capital/interest, land/rent, labour/wages, entrepreneur/profit) with the Islamic framework, where money-capital cannot earn interest and 'profit' is a residual reward tied to risk-bearing, not a guaranteed return.","simpleExplanation":"In standard economics, capital earns interest just for being lent, regardless of outcome. In Islamic economics, that's not allowed — money itself isn't 'capital' that earns rent or interest; it only becomes productive when actually invested in a real business, and its owner only earns a 'profit' if the business succeeds — and bears the loss if it doesn't. Land can be rented (since it isn't consumed/altered by use), but money can't be, since it changes form the moment it's used.","academicExplanation":"Distribution of wealth must ensure no factor of production is deprived of its due share nor exploits others; land, labour and capital jointly create value, so the landowner, labourer and capital owner should jointly share the produce — with the Islamic system's distinctive feature being that capital must also bear any loss. Zakat is retained as compulsory support for those unable to contribute to production due to a handicap. Capitalism's four factors: (1) Capital (produced means of production; compensated by interest); (2) Land (all natural resources used without prior human processing; compensated by rent); (3) Labour (human effort; compensated by wages); (4) Entrepreneur/organization (combines the other three, bears profit/loss risk; compensated by profit). Islamic economics reframes this: capital (means of production consumed/altered during the production process, so cannot fetch rent) — its compensation is 'profit', but profit comes bundled with responsibility/liability: it is the residual revenue after paying all other parties, and if the residual is negative, the capital owner bears the loss (shortfall in principal). Land (means of production whose corpus/original form remains unaltered in use) can be rented/leased, earning rental. Labour (physical/mental exertion, including organization/planning) earns wages. Crucially, money itself is not recognized as capital in the Islamic framework and cannot earn a profit or rent purely by being lent, since it is consumed/changes form upon use; a fund provider who bears the loss risk is thereby also an entrepreneur, earning profit/loss on capital plus wages/remuneration if managing the business themselves, or (if providing capital to another to manage) a share of profit as capital-owner while the manager earns a 'wage' via a profit share — but if the business loses, the capital owner bears the loss while the manager's labour goes unrewarded. If money capital is instead lent as a loan (Qard), the entrepreneur must repay exactly the principal regardless of profit or loss; if capital is provided to a business seeking a profit share, any loss pro-rata erodes that capital, obliging the provider to accept the shortfall. This yields five practical factors of production: land, capital, labour, management, and responsibility/liability — the last (Dhaman) may be borne by an individual or a partnership group, who may subcontract work to others, sharing the resulting profits; the reward for coordinating/completing a contracted job is also profit.","examExplanation":"Capitalism's four factors and their rewards: Capital→interest, Land→rent, Labour→wages, Entrepreneur→profit. Islamic economics: money/capital cannot earn interest or rent (it is consumed/altered on use); its reward ('profit') is a residual claim after all other parties are paid, contingent on business success, and the capital owner bears any loss. Land can be rented (its form is unaltered by use). Labour earns wages. A capital provider who bears risk is thereby also an entrepreneur. A Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal amount regardless of the borrower's profit or loss — no interest is chargeable either way.","keyPoints":["Capitalism's four factors and rewards: Capital→interest, Land→rent, Labour→wages, Entrepreneur/organization→profit.","In Islamic economics, money-capital cannot earn interest or rent — it is consumed/altered when used, unlike land, which retains its form and can be rented.","'Profit' in Islamic economics is a residual reward after paying all other claimants; it comes bundled with liability — if the residual is negative, the capital owner bears the loss.","A capital provider who bears risk of loss is thereby considered also an entrepreneur; if they hire a manager instead, the manager earns a profit-share ('wage'), but earns nothing if the business loses (their labour is 'wasted').","A Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal, irrespective of the borrower's profit or loss — this is distinct from profit-sharing capital contribution, where the provider's capital is pro-rata eroded by any loss.","Islamic economics effectively recognizes five factors of production: land, capital, labour, management, and responsibility/liability (Dhaman)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Profit (Islamic framework)","definition":"The residual revenue of a business after paying all other parties (wages, rentals, expenses); a reward for capital productivity and/or workmanship/responsibility-bearing, not a guaranteed return on capital or enterprise per se."}],"conditions":["Money capital cannot earn rent or interest, since it is consumed or changes form when used (unlike land, whose corpus remains unaltered).","A capital provider is entitled to profit only if there is a positive residual after paying wages, rentals and other business expenses; otherwise the shortfall in principal is borne by the capital owner."],"principles":["A capital provider or entrepreneur is not entitled to profit simply by virtue of being a capital owner or entrepreneur — entitlement depends on the actual outcome (residual) of the business and the nature of the agreement."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Entrepreneur combining land, labour and own capital","kind":"textbook","text":"An entrepreneur who brings together land, labour and machinery, using his own money capital, must pay wages and rental for land/machinery per agreed terms; he earns a profit on capital or reward for entrepreneurship only if a residual remains after paying rental, wages and other expenses."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume 'capital' in Islamic economics is simply barred from earning any return; the actual rule is more precise — money-capital alone (lent without risk) cannot earn interest, but capital invested at risk in a genuine business can earn 'profit' as a residual, risk-bearing reward."],"distinctions":["Land (retains form, can be rented for a fixed rental) vs Capital/money (consumed or altered on use, cannot be rented — can only earn a residual, risk-contingent profit).","Qard (loan, principal repayable exactly regardless of outcome) vs profit-sharing capital contribution (provider's capital pro-rata bears any loss)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Mudarabah","Musharakah","Dhaman"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,36,37]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Capital (consumed/altered in use)","b":"Land/assets (corpus unaltered)","text":"Capital earns profit with liability for loss; assets whose form remains intact can be leased and earn rent."}],"table":{"caption":"Factors of production and their rewards (Section 2.5.3)","head":["Factor","Capitalism","Islamic economics"],"rows":[["Capital","Interest","Profit — residual, with liability for loss; no rent"],["Land (and non-consumable assets)","Rent","Rent"],["Labour","Wages","Wages"],["Entrepreneur / organisation","Profit","Profit as reward for responsibility/workmanship; management and responsibility treated as factors"]]}},{"id":"t2.5.4","section":"2.5.4","title":"Restrained Individual Freedom","pages":[37,38],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["general-permissibility","riba","gharar","maisir"],"intuition":"Laissez-faire says “do what you like”. Islam says “do anything not prohibited — but not at others’ expense”.","simpleExplanation":"Unbridled profit motive distorts markets. Islam imposes checks on consumer behaviour and wealth-seeking, but everything not prohibited is allowed. The key economic prohibitions are interest, hazard (Gharar) and gambling.","academicExplanation":["Laissez-faire has built-in distortions from the unbridled profit motive; secular State intervention also fails because profit without ethics finds loopholes, and socially undesirable industries (e.g. gambling) become part of policy while capital avoids education, health, housing and commodity production.","Islamic economics balances production and consumption and cares about distribution, drawing a line between lawful and unlawful. It is religion-based, valuation-oriented and spiritually bound — both positive and normative. Its scope is the administration of scarce resources in the light of the Islamic ethical concept of welfare. All work except that leading to indecency or loss to others is permissible; what is not prohibited is permissible. The most important economic prohibitions are interest, hazard and gambling, because of their harmful impacts."],"examExplanation":"Laissez-faire and secular intervention both fail without ethics. Islam: freedom within limits; permissibility the rule; key prohibitions — interest, hazard (Gharar), gambling. Scope: administering scarce resources per Islamic ethical welfare.","keyPoints":["Scope of Islamic economics: administration of scarce resources in the light of the ethical concept of welfare.","Three most important economic prohibitions: interest, hazard, gambling."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Restraint on a profitable business","kind":"practice","text":"A casino may be highly profitable and legal in a secular system, but Islamic economics excludes it because gambling is one of the three key economic prohibitions, with interest and hazard (Gharar) (pp. 37–38)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Secular State intervention is enough to fix market failures.","right":"Without ethical norms the profit motive finds loopholes; socially harmful industries become part of policy and capital avoids education, health and housing (p. 37)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2","t5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which three prohibitions does the author call the most important in economics?","options":["Interest, hazard and gambling","Trade, leasing and partnership","Taxes, rent and wages","Zakat, Hisbah and Ijtihad"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Interest, hazard and gambling (p. 38)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.5.4","sectionTitle":"Restrained Individual Freedom","title":"Restrained Individual Freedom: Critique of Laissez-Faire and Secular State Intervention","covers":["t2.5.4"],"overview":"The author critiques both unrestrained laissez-faire capitalism and secular State intervention as inadequate without an ethical/moral filter, and identifies the prohibition of interest, hazard (Gharar) and gambling as Islamic economics' most important curbs on economic behaviour.","simpleExplanation":"Pure free-market capitalism, if left unchecked by ethics, allows the profit motive to justify even harmful industries like gambling. But the author also says a secular government stepping in isn't automatically better, because without a moral compass, state intervention just creates its own loopholes for corruption. Islamic economics instead sets clear ethical boundaries (especially banning interest, excessive uncertainty and gambling) so that the search for profit stays within a framework of right and wrong.","academicExplanation":"The author argues that laissez-faire's built-in flaw is the unbridled 'profit motive', focused on enrichment without regard to social impact; while the Great Depression and Keynesian revolution temporarily undermined faith in pure market forces, recent disenchantment with large government has restored calls for minimal-intervention liberalism. However, secular State intervention is equally inadequate on its own, since a profit motive without ethical norms finds loopholes for injustice/corruption regardless of who exercises it — the author notes that socially undesirable industries (gambling, sex-related industries) can even become part of public policy under a purely secular calculus, diverting capital from socially desirable sectors (education, health, housing, agriculture, industry). Islamic economics is not anti-money/anti-market, but maintains balance between production and consumption, cares about distribution, and draws a clear line between lawful (Halal) and unlawful (Haram) — individuals are not left entirely free in their economic choices; basic rules govern the intensity of wealth-gaining and income-consuming behaviour. It is simultaneously positive and normative, linking material and moral requirements. The general permissibility principle applies (anything not prohibited is allowed), but the most important prohibitions specifically in the economic sphere are interest, hazard (Gharar) and gambling (Maisir/Qimar), due to their extremely harmful social impact — these limitations serve the overall Maqasid of Shari'ah for a materially and spiritually satisfied society.","examExplanation":"Neither pure laissez-faire (profit motive with no ethical constraint) nor secular State intervention (equally prone to corruption/loopholes absent moral grounding) is adequate on its own, per the author. Islamic economics restrains individual freedom via a clear Halal/Haram boundary and basic rules on wealth-gaining/consumption intensity, while still permitting anything not expressly prohibited (General Permissibility). The three most important economic prohibitions, due to their extremely harmful social impact, are: interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar), and gambling/games of chance (Maisir/Qimar) — detailed fully in Chapter 3.","keyPoints":["Laissez-faire's structural flaw, per the author: an unbridled profit motive pursued without regard to impact on others or society.","Secular State intervention is not automatically a fix — without ethical norms, the profit motive still finds loopholes for injustice/corruption, and can even legitimize socially harmful industries (e.g. gambling) as public policy.","Islamic economics restrains, but does not eliminate, individual economic freedom — it draws a clear Halal/Haram line and imposes basic rules on wealth-gaining and consumption intensity.","General Permissibility still applies: any activity/commodity not expressly prohibited by Shari'ah texts is permissible.","The most important prohibitions specifically in the economic sphere are interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar), and gambling/games of chance (Maisir/Qimar) — due to their severe harm to society."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Economic freedom in Islam is restrained, not eliminated: individuals may pursue any activity not expressly prohibited (General Permissibility), but the profit motive must operate within Shari'ah-defined ethical limits."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["The author's critique of laissez-faire is not an endorsement of unlimited secular State intervention either — he explicitly argues both are inadequate without an ethical/moral foundation; Islamic economics is presented as a third path grounded in Shari'ah-defined limits."],"distinctions":["Laissez-faire (unrestrained profit motive, no ethical filter) vs secular State intervention (also prone to corruption absent ethics) vs Islamic economics (market-based but ethically bounded by Halal/Haram limits, especially the prohibitions of Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Maisir/Qimar","Halal","Haram"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37,38]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Laissez-faire","b":"Islamic restrained freedom","text":"Laissez-faire is neutral between ends; Islamic economics is value-oriented, draws a line between lawful and unlawful and imposes checks on consumer and business behaviour (p. 37)."}]},{"id":"t2.5.5","section":"2.5.5","title":"Liberalism versus State Intervention","pages":[38,41],"tier":"core","concepts":["hisbah","state-role"],"intuition":"Should the State stay out of markets (Pareto efficiency) or intervene? The author argues for an overseeing, facilitating State.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional economics assumes free markets produce efficiency and equity (Pareto optimality) — but its assumptions rarely hold. Islam accepts demand and supply but gives the State an overseeing role: preventing exploitation, maintaining market health, redistributing when needed and ensuring stable money.","academicExplanation":["Pareto optimality assumes harmony between individual preferences and social interest, equal distribution, prices reflecting urgency of wants, and perfect competition. As no real market satisfies these, prices distort priorities; the concept does not fit Islamic philosophy and is biased against normative goals when prices alone allocate resources.","The Prophet’s parable of the boat (reported by Bukhari) shows society cannot be a silent spectator to harmful individual acts. The State is recognised to prevent exploitation; rulers lose the right to obedience if they transgress Shari’ah. Islam accepts demand and supply subject to limits; the ultimate objective is social justice. Four types of government action: (1) ensure compliance with the Islamic code by education and, if needed, compulsion; (2) maintain healthy market conditions; (3) modify allocation and distribution through guidance, regulation or direct intervention; (4) positive steps in production and capital formation. Caliph Umar asked a seller undercutting the market to raise his price or leave.","Ibnul Qayyim: lawful government policies based on justice are part of Shari’ah. The State must recover usurped wealth; Hisbah checks irregularities. Chapra’s functions of the State: eradicating poverty, law and order, full employment and optimum growth; economic planning; social and economic justice; stability in the value of money (erosion of money’s value likened to corrupting the world, citing 6:152 and 7:85); international relations and defence. Regulators must protect small savers, prevent cartels, and set policy so that the expected rate of return from real-sector business becomes the benchmark for allocating funds; monetary growth should be adequate, not excessive or deficient."],"examExplanation":"Pareto optimality relies on unrealistic assumptions → biased against normative goals. Islam: demand–supply accepted with limits; State oversight. Four government actions; Chapra’s five State functions incl. stable value of money. Real-sector return should be benchmark for fund allocation.","keyPoints":["Parable of the boat: society must prevent harmful individual acts.","Four types of government action in economic life.","Chapra’s five functions of the State.","Stability in the value of money is a State duty.","Real-sector expected return as benchmark for allocation of funds.","Chapra’s five State functions: eradicate poverty and ensure employment and growth; economic planning; social and economic justice; stable value of money; international relations and defence (pp. 40–41).","Regulators should make the expected rate of return from real-sector business the benchmark for allocating funds (p. 41)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The parable of the boat","kind":"textbook","text":"Passengers in the hold want to drill a hole to get water; if those on deck do not stop them, all drown. Society cannot be a silent spectator to harmful individual acts (p. 38)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Islamic State should run businesses directly.","right":"The State’s role is overseeing and facilitating; Ibn Khaldun warned against State monopoly in commerce. Direct intervention is a last resort to restore balance."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.4","t4.7","t18.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is one of Chapra’s functions of the State listed in the chapter?","options":["Setting interest rates for banks","Stability in the value of money","Owning all factories","Abolishing markets"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Stability in the value of money is listed among the State’s functions (p. 40)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch2-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch2-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"2.5.5","sectionTitle":"Liberalism versus State Intervention","title":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","covers":["t2.5.5"],"overview":"The author rejects conventional Pareto efficiency as a fit for Islamic economics (since its underlying assumptions rarely hold in the real world), and details the active, justice-oriented role the Islamic State must play in overseeing the economy.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional economics assumes markets left alone will produce a fair and efficient outcome (Pareto efficiency), but this rests on unrealistic assumptions (perfect competition, prices always reflecting true value, equal starting wealth). Since real markets don't meet these conditions, the author argues the State must play an active role — not to run businesses, but to prevent exploitation, keep the market fair, eradicate poverty, ensure justice, and keep the value of money stable — while still respecting people's freedom to trade, and only intervening against genuinely harmful abuses.","academicExplanation":"Conventional economics treats individual self-interest as leading to unrestrained wealth/want maximization, assuming markets create 'order', 'harmony', 'efficiency' and 'equity' on their own, so government should abstain from intervening; the Pareto efficiency/optimality concept assumes markets automatically achieve 'equity' via market equilibrium, and any intervention reduces efficiency. But this rests on assumptions (harmony of individual and social interest, equal income/wealth distribution, prices truly reflecting the urgency of wants, perfect competition) that no real-world market satisfies — creating a built-in bias against normative goals if allocation relies primarily on prices. The author instead frames society/individuals as accountable to God for their conduct, illustrated by the Prophet's parable (reported by Imam Bukhari) of two groups sharing a boat, where those in the lower deck drilling a hole to fetch water (for their own convenience) sink everyone unless those above intervene — illustrating that society cannot be a silent spectator to harmful individual acts. The State (social authority) is recognized in Islam to prevent exploitation/moral degeneration and to promote material/spiritual interests (Qur'an 4:59 on obeying those in authority — but note obedience to rulers is void where it entails disobedience to Allah, per Hadith). The State can introduce laws for social justice, subject to a historical example: Caliph Umar Farooq requiring a trader selling below market rate to either raise the price or leave the market (protecting fair market functioning). Ibnul Qayyim (Ilamul Muwaqqi'in) is quoted that lawful government policy — provided grounded in justice and fair play — is itself part of Shari'ah, regardless of terminology. The literature identifies four types of government economic action: (1) ensuring compliance with the Islamic code of conduct (education, and compulsion where necessary); (2) maintaining healthy market conditions; (3) modifying resource allocation/income distribution via guidance/regulation and direct intervention/participation if needed; (4) taking positive steps in production/capital formation to accelerate growth. Chapra lists five State functions: eradication of poverty/full employment/optimum growth; economic planning; ensuring social/economic justice; stability in the value of money (grounded in Qur'an 6:152, 7:85 on giving full measure/weight, framing currency debasement/erosion as 'corrupting the world'); and harmonizing international relations/national defence. The Hisbah institution is retained to check irregularities and must be run by people of high integrity; the Qur'an's disapproval of wealth concentration (59:7) and emphasis on justice (14:90) underlie the State's duty to recover illegally usurped wealth. Regulators/central banks must ensure banking/financial institutions function smoothly, protecting small savers/the masses from cartels/monopolies and preventing antisocial activity, with fiscal/monetary policy calibrated so real-sector rates of return (not speculative signals) guide efficient fund allocation.","examExplanation":"The author rejects Pareto efficiency as an appropriate framework for Islamic economics, since its assumptions (perfect competition, equal wealth distribution, prices truly reflecting wants) rarely hold in reality, biasing outcomes against normative goals. The Islamic State's role is active but not commercial: four government functions (ensure Shari'ah-compliant conduct; maintain healthy market conditions; modify allocation/distribution via regulation or direct intervention if needed; accelerate growth via production/capital formation). Chapra's five State functions: poverty eradication/full employment/growth; economic planning; social/economic justice; monetary value stability; and international relations/defence. Al-Hisbah remains the key institution for checking market irregularities. The Prophet's 'boat parable' illustrates why society cannot be a passive bystander to individually harmful economic acts.","keyPoints":["The author rejects the conventional Pareto efficiency concept for Islamic economics, since its assumptions (perfect competition, equal wealth distribution, prices reflecting true wants) rarely hold in real markets.","The Prophet's parable of two groups sharing a boat (one drilling a hole in the lower deck) illustrates why society cannot remain a silent spectator to harmful individual economic acts.","Obedience to rulers is conditional: 'Obedience (to rulers) is not valid where a disobedience to Allah is involved.'","Four types of government economic action: ensuring Shari'ah-compliant conduct; maintaining healthy market conditions; modifying resource allocation/distribution (via regulation or direct intervention); accelerating growth via production/capital formation.","Chapra's five State functions: poverty eradication/full employment/optimum growth; economic planning; social/economic justice; stability in the value of money; harmonizing international relations and national defence.","Al-Hisbah (the ombudsman institution) checks market irregularities and must be run by people of high integrity; the State must recover illegally usurped wealth (Qur'an 59:7 disapproves of wealth concentration).","Caliph Umar Farooq's historical intervention (requiring a below-market seller to raise price or leave) illustrates permissible State action to protect fair market functioning."],"definitions":[{"term":"Pareto efficiency (as critiqued)","definition":"A conventional economic concept assuming market equilibrium automatically achieves an optimal, equitable outcome; the author argues its underlying assumptions (perfect competition, equal wealth distribution, prices reflecting true wants) do not hold in real markets, making it unsuitable as an Islamic economics framework."}],"conditions":["Government intervention (price-setting, market discipline) is warranted specifically against monopolistic, unjust or exploitative practices — not against ordinary market-driven price fluctuations.","Obedience to State authority is conditional on the ruler not commanding disobedience to Allah."],"principles":["Lawful government policy, when grounded in justice and fair play, is itself considered part of Shari'ah, regardless of the terminology used to describe it (per Ibnul Qayyim).","Stability in the value of money is a matter of social justice, not merely technical monetary policy — continuous erosion of money's real value is characterized (per Qur'an 6:152, 7:85) as 'corrupting the world'."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Caliph Umar Farooq and the below-market seller","kind":"textbook","text":"Caliph Umar Farooq asked a trader selling a commodity well below the market price to either raise the price or leave the market, illustrating a permissible State intervention to protect fair market functioning."},{"title":"The Prophet's parable of the boat","kind":"textbook","text":"As reported by Imam Bukhari: those who keep within Allah's limits and those who transgress are like two groups sharing a boat, one on the upper deck and one in the hold. Those in the hold, tired of going up for water, consider drilling a hole in the bottom for their own convenience — if those above do not stop them, all drown; if they do, all are saved."}],"commonConfusions":["The State's 'active role' does not mean running businesses directly — the four government functions and Chapra's five functions focus on regulation, planning, justice and stability, not direct State commercial operation (consistent with the more limited State-intervention view attributed to Ibn Khaldun in 2.3.1)."],"distinctions":["Pareto efficiency's assumption-dependent 'automatic' market equity (rejected as unrealistic) vs the Islamic framework's explicit, active State role in ensuring justice where market assumptions fail to hold."],"relatedConcepts":["Al-Hisbah","Distributive justice","Money and monetary policy"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,39,40,41,42]}}],"processSteps":["Ensure compliance with the Islamic code (education; compulsion if needed)","Maintain healthy market conditions","Modify allocation and distribution (guide, regulate, intervene if needed)","Take positive steps in production and capital formation"]}],"questions":[{"id":"q2.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair are the PRIMARY sources of Shari’ah?","options":["Ijma‘a and Qiyas","Qur’an and Sunnah","‘Urf and Maslaha","Ijtihad and Hisbah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Revelation — the Qur’an and Sunnah — is the primary source; Ijma‘a and Qiyas are secondary.","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the sources of Shari’ah","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.2","type":"tf","prompt":"Under the rule of General Permissibility, every commercial practice must be expressly approved in the Qur’an or Sunnah before it can be used.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The opposite: in socio-economic matters all acts and things not expressly prohibited are permissible.","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Ibahatul Asliyah","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.3","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all PRIMARY objectives of Shari’ah listed by the author.","options":["Protection of life","Protection of property","Maintenance of peace and security","Protection of intellect","Protection of honour"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4],"explanation":"Peace and security is a secondary objective; the primary are religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour.","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the Maqasid","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.4","type":"match","prompt":"Match each factor of production to its reward in Islamic economics.","pairs":[["Capital","Profit (residual), bearing loss"],["Land and non-consumable assets","Rent"],["Labour","Wages"]],"explanation":"Section 2.5.3: capital earns profit with liability; rent is for assets whose corpus remains; wages for labour.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare rewards of factors of production","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.5","type":"scenario","prompt":"An investor gives money to an entrepreneur and demands a fixed annual return whether or not the business makes a profit. According to Section 2.5.3, what is wrong with this?","options":["Nothing; capital deserves a fixed reward","Money capital can earn only a profit as a residual and must bear loss; a fixed return on money is interest","It should be called rent","The entrepreneur must bear the capital loss alone"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Capital earns profit only as a residual with liability for loss; money cannot earn a fixed return in itself.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the Islamic theory of capital","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.6","type":"identify","prompt":"Which institution was empowered to check monopolistic and unjust pricing practices?","options":["Zakat","Hisbah (ombudsman)","Waqf","Bait-ul-Mal"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Al-Hisbah — the ombudsman (p. 31).","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the role of Hisbah","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts positive and normative economics?","options":["Positive economics prescribes policy; normative only describes","Positive economics is value-neutral and describes; normative involves value judgements","Both are value-neutral","Islamic economics is purely positive"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Positive describes, normative prescribes with value judgements (p. 30).","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish positive and normative economics","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.8","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why, according to the author, does Pareto optimality not fit Islamic economic philosophy?","options":["It is too religious","Its assumptions (harmony of interests, equal distribution, prices reflecting urgency, perfect competition) rarely hold, biasing it against normative goals","It requires State ownership","It prohibits markets"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 2.5.5.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Critique Pareto efficiency","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.9","type":"order","prompt":"Order the responsibility for fulfilling the basic needs of a person who cannot earn, as described in Section 2.5.2.","items":["The capable individual works to meet own and dependents’ needs","Relatives and neighbours support the person","The State supports the person"],"explanation":"Each capable person must work; if unable, fellow beings (relatives, neighbours) support; failing that, the State.","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the welfare approach","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.10","type":"application","prompt":"A trader sells far below the market price to drive others out. Which precedent in Chapter 2 shows the State may act?","options":["Ibn Khaldun’s tax theory","Caliph Umar asking a seller underpricing the market to raise the price or leave","The parable of the boat","Mannan’s imperatives"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Umar Farooq asked such a seller to increase the price or leave the market (p. 40).","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the State’s regulatory role","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.11","type":"definition","prompt":"“Welfare in this world and the Hereafter” is the meaning of:","options":["Falah","Israf","Dhaman","Taqwa"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Falah (p. 35).","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define key terms","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why the author says profit is not a reward for capital or enterprise per se.","keywords":["residual","loss","responsibility","productivity"],"correctAnswer":"Profit results from the productivity of invested capital or rewards workmanship or shouldering responsibility. It is a residual after wages, rent and expenses; if the business loses, the capital provider bears the shortfall. So mere ownership of capital or being an entrepreneur does not entitle one to profit.","explanation":"See Section 2.5.3.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain entitlement to profit","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.13","type":"tf","prompt":"The author states that Islam’s limits on acquiring wealth concern the quality/means of acquisition rather than the quantity of wealth.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Section 2.5.1.","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain property rights","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33,34]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.14","type":"match","prompt":"Match each branch of Shari’ah injunctions to what it covers.","pairs":[["Aqaid","Belief and worship"],["Akhlaq","Disciplining the self"],["Ahkam","Socio-economic and legal systems"],["Nawahi","Prohibitions"]],"explanation":"p. 21.","topicId":"t2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the branches of Shari’ah","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2","pages":[21,21]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.15","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, lapses in respect of the rights of human beings can be forgiven only by the aggrieved person.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Allah may forgive lapses in His rights; rights of people are forgiven by the aggrieved (p. 22).","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish rights of Allah and of people","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.16","type":"identify","prompt":"Which tool refers to “prevalent practice” that jurists consider when deciding the Shari’ah position of contracts?","options":["Maslaha-e-Mursalah","‘Urf","Qiyas","Ijma‘a"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 22.","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify secondary tools of jurists","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.17","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are SECONDARY objectives of Shari’ah? (Select all.)","options":["Establishment of justice and equity","Protection of progeny","Social security and mutual help","Maintenance of peace and security","Protection of intellect"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3],"explanation":"Progeny and intellect are primary objectives (pp. 23–24).","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish primary and secondary objectives","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.18","type":"scenario","prompt":"A policy protects small savers from misleading product disclosures. Which socio-economic right listed in Chapter 2 does it most directly serve?","options":["Right to be informed","Right to education","Right to a healthy environment","Right to be heard"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Eight rights derived from the Qur’an and Sunnah (p. 25).","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the socio-economic rights","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.19","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which verse does the author use to show Islam’s integrated approach to worship and economic life?","options":["2:177","2:275","59:7","6:152"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 25: righteousness includes spending on kin, orphans, the needy and fulfilling contracts.","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the textual basis for integration","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[25,27]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.20","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many socio-economic imperatives for studying Islamic economics does M.A. Mannan list?","options":["Three","Five","Seven","Nine"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international (p. 26).","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Mannan’s imperatives","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[25,27]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["q-ch2-8"],"explanationAlt":"Mannan identifies seven imperatives: ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical and international."},{"id":"q2.21","type":"application","prompt":"A student claims Ibn Khaldun favoured state monopolies in commerce. How should you correct him?","options":["He did favour them for revenue","He supported private enterprise and held that state participation in commerce and agriculture ruins revenue and the economy","He never wrote on the State","He supported monopolies only in agriculture"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 27–28.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Ibn Khaldun’s views","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,30]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.22","type":"identify","prompt":"Which scholar discussed Thaman-e-mithl (normal market price or wage) and the role of the ombudsman?","options":["Ibn Khaldun","Ibn Taymiyah","Nasiruddin Tusi","Shah Waliullah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 28.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify classical contributions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,30]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.23","type":"tf","prompt":"The author states that contemporary Islamic economists have already built a comprehensive quantified model of the Islamic economy.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The work consists of segments not yet put together; value judgements make quantification difficult (pp. 29–30).","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the state of the discipline","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,30]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.24","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why does the author compare distribution in an Islamic economy to blood in the human body?","options":["Wealth should be distributed exactly equally","Distribution should be equitable, not equal — like blood reaching organs according to their different functions","Wealth should stay with the State","Distribution does not matter"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 30–31.","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain equitable versus equal distribution","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.25","type":"scenario","prompt":"Prices of a staple rise only because of changes in supply and demand. Following the Prophet’s practice described in Chapter 2, what should the authority do?","options":["Fix the price immediately","Not intervene while fluctuations are due to market forces alone; act through Hisbah only against monopolistic or unjust practices","Ban the product","Impose a tax"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 31.","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on price intervention","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.26","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are criteria of the integrated Islamic social framework listed in Section 2.4.1? (Select all.)","options":["Equilibrium between work and worship","Distributive justice","Unlimited individual sovereignty","Freedom of conscience","The principle of co-existence"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4],"explanation":"Sovereignty of individuals is limited, not unlimited (p. 32).","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the framework criteria","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[31,32]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.27","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT listed among the key macroeconomic features of an ideal Islamic economy?","options":["Abolition of Riba","Application of Zakat","Avoidance of Israf","Fixed government-set prices for all goods"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"p. 33.","topicId":"t2.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Recall macro features of an Islamic economy","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5","pages":[32,33]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.28","type":"tf","prompt":"Islam limits how much wealth a person may accumulate, in terms of quantity.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Limits concern the quality or means of acquiring, not quantity (p. 33).","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand limits on acquisition","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33,34]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.29","type":"order","prompt":"Order who is responsible for meeting the basic needs of a person unable to earn, as described in Section 2.5.2.","items":["The person himself, by working","Fellow beings — relatives and neighbours","The State"],"explanation":"pp. 34–35.","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence responsibility for need fulfilment","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.30","type":"application","prompt":"An entrepreneur uses his own money, hires labour and rents a machine. After paying wages and rent, nothing is left. What profit does he earn?","options":["A fixed return on his money","None — profit is only the residual after all payments","Interest at the market rate","Half of the wages"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Profit is residual; money itself is not capital that earns in itself (p. 36).","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the residual nature of profit","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.31","type":"comparison","prompt":"In Islamic economics, why can money not earn rent?","options":["It is too valuable","It is consumed and changes form when used, unlike land or machinery","Rent is always prohibited","Only the State can charge rent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 36.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse why money cannot be rented","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.32","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Section 2.5.4, what are the most important prohibitions in the field of economics?","options":["Interest, hazard and gambling","Trade, lease and partnership","Taxes and tariffs","Advertising and marketing"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 38.","topicId":"t2.5.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the key prohibitions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37,38]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.33","type":"identify","prompt":"The parable of passengers drilling a hole in a boat illustrates which principle?","options":["Freedom of contract","Society cannot stay silent when individual acts endanger it","General permissibility","Private property"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Hadith in Bukhari (p. 38).","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Interpret the boat parable","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q2.34","type":"short","prompt":"Why does the author regard stability in the value of money as a State duty?","keywords":["measure of value","erosion","full measure","justice"],"correctAnswer":"Money is a measure of value; continuous erosion of its real value harms social justice and welfare and can be seen, in light of the Qur’anic command to give full measure and weight, as corrupting the world. The State should contribute what it can to price stability.","explanation":"Chapra’s list of State functions (pp. 40–41).","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the State’s monetary duty","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch2-1","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the five branches of Shari'ah discussed in Chapter 2?","explanation":"Shari'ah's branches, per the chapter, are Aqaid (belief/worship), Akhlaq (self-discipline), Ahkam (socio-economic/legal systems), Fraidh (obligations), and Nawahi (prohibitions). Riba and Mudarabah are specific finance concepts, not branches of Shari'ah itself.","topicId":"t2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2","topicLabel":"Shari'ah: Definition, Branches and Sources","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Shari'ah and list its primary and secondary sources","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2","pages":[21]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Aqaid (belief/worship)","Riba (interest)","Akhlaq (self-discipline)","Fraidh (obligations)","Mudarabah (profit-sharing)"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch2-2","type":"order","prompt":"Order these sources of Shari'ah from primary to secondary, per Chapter 2's hierarchy.","explanation":"The Qur'an and Sunnah are the primary sources (revelation); Ijma'a and Qiyas (both derived through Ijtihad) are secondary sources used to address emerging issues not directly covered by the primary sources.","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","topicLabel":"Shari'ah: Definition, Branches and Sources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Shari'ah and list its primary and secondary sources","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"items":["Qur'an and Sunnah","Ijma'a (consensus)","Qiyas (analogy, via Ijtihad)"]},{"id":"q-ch2-3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A modern financial instrument is not explicitly addressed in the Qur'an, Sunnah, or existing Ijma'a. What principle governs whether it is permissible, per Chapter 2?","explanation":"For socio-economic (Mu'amalat) matters, the governing principle is General Permissibility: all acts/things not expressly prohibited by the original Shari'ah sources are permissible.","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","topicLabel":"Shari'ah: Definition, Branches and Sources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Shari'ah and list its primary and secondary sources","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It is automatically prohibited until explicitly permitted","General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah) — it is permissible unless expressly prohibited by the original sources of Shari'ah","It must be voted on by a majority of Muslim-majority countries","Only the State can decide, regardless of Shari'ah sources"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-4","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per Chapter 2, lapses in the rights of fellow human beings can be forgiven directly by Allah, without needing the aggrieved person's forgiveness.","explanation":"The chapter states that while Allah may forgive lapses regarding His own rights, lapses regarding the rights of human beings must be forgiven by the aggrieved person(s).","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","topicLabel":"Shari'ah: Definition, Branches and Sources","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Shari'ah and list its primary and secondary sources","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch2-5","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are among the six Primary Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah?","explanation":"The six Primary Objectives are protection/preservation of Religion, Life, Progeny, Property, Intellect, and Honour. Profit maximization and a specific growth rate are not among them.","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","topicLabel":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: Primary and Secondary","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the primary (Maqasid) and secondary objectives of Shari'ah and explain their relevance to economics","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,23]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Religion","Profit maximization","Life","Property","Economic growth rate"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch2-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Chapra (as cited by Ayub), what must 'well-being' include beyond materialist/hedonist measures?","explanation":"Chapra argues well-being must incorporate humanitarian and spiritual goals (human brotherhood, socio-economic justice, mental peace, family/social harmony) alongside material tests like social equality, need fulfilment and economic stability.","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","topicLabel":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: Primary and Secondary","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the primary (Maqasid) and secondary objectives of Shari'ah and explain their relevance to economics","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[24]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only GDP growth rates","Humanitarian and spiritual goals such as family/social harmony, alongside social equality and need fulfilment","Only stock market performance","Nothing beyond material consumption"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-7","type":"short","prompt":"Define Falah as used in Chapter 2.","explanation":"Falah refers to comprehensive welfare spanning both this world and the Hereafter, contrasted with the narrower 'pleasure' objective of positive/secular economics.","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","topicLabel":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: Primary and Secondary","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the primary (Maqasid) and secondary objectives of Shari'ah and explain their relevance to economics","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[23]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Welfare/success in this world and the Hereafter — the Islamic concept of true happiness/well-being, distinct from mere worldly pleasure."},{"id":"q-ch2-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does Ayub say has replaced the Western concept of the welfare state in recent decades, contributing to persistent poverty despite growth?","explanation":"Ayub argues the Western welfare state concept has been replaced by minimal-government ideology ('the least government is the best government'), with State intervention against income disparities becoming more myth than reality.","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.3","topicLabel":"Why Study Islamic Economics? The Integrated Islamic Worldview","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why the author argues Islamic economics should be studied","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[26]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Full government ownership of all industry","'The least government is the best government' ideology, with the market as the engine of growth","Universal basic income programs","Return to the gold standard"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-10","type":"identify","prompt":"Identify the classical Muslim scholar (1332-1406) whose Muqaddamah is credited with anticipating Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes.","explanation":"Ibn Khaldun (1332-1406), author of the Muqaddamah, is credited with anticipating Adam Smith's labour value theory, Malthus's population theory, and Keynes's normative State role.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","topicLabel":"Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify key classical Muslim scholars (e.g. Ibn Khaldun, Ibn Taymiyah) and their economic contributions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ibn Taymiyah","Ibn Khaldun","Al-Ghazali","Ibn Qayyim"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"What did Ibn Khaldun warn happens when a State's power becomes absolute?","explanation":"Ibn Khaldun warned that the growth of absolute State power is a cause of economic decline, since it must be preserved by expanding bureaucracy, army and police, supported by increased taxation, confiscation and direct State interference in commerce/industry.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","topicLabel":"Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify key classical Muslim scholars (e.g. Ibn Khaldun, Ibn Taymiyah) and their economic contributions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Economic prosperity increases indefinitely","It causes economic decline, since absolute power requires expanding bureaucracy/army/police funded by increased taxation and confiscation","It has no effect on the economy","It automatically reduces taxation"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-12","type":"short","prompt":"What concept did Ibn Taymiyah develop relating to fair pricing?","explanation":"Ibn Taymiyah discussed Thaman-e-mithl (normal/market price or wage), along with economic freedom, market pricing, the ombudsman's role, and government functions.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","topicLabel":"Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify key classical Muslim scholars (e.g. Ibn Khaldun, Ibn Taymiyah) and their economic contributions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[29]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Thaman-e-mithl — the normal/market price that has to be paid when there is an issue in deciding the actual price of a transaction."},{"id":"q-ch2-13","type":"comparison","prompt":"Contrast positive economics with normative economics as presented in Chapter 2.","explanation":"Positive economics (per Lionel Robbins) is value-neutral, describing behaviour under scarcity without prescribing. Normative/welfare economics necessarily involves value judgements and policy prescriptions; Islamic economics is normative.","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","topicLabel":"Positive versus Normative Economics in the Islamic Framework","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish positive (economics proper) from normative (welfare) economics in the Islamic framework","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Positive economics is value-neutral and descriptive; normative economics involves value judgements and is prescriptive","Positive economics is prescriptive; normative economics is purely descriptive","They are identical concepts with different names","Positive economics applies only to Islamic economies; normative economics applies only to capitalist economies"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch2-14","type":"scenario","prompt":"Market prices for a commodity rise due to a genuine, temporary supply shortage caused by weather, with no evidence of hoarding or manipulation. Based on the Prophet's (pbuh) approach described in Chapter 2, should the State fix prices?","explanation":"The chapter states the Prophet categorically discouraged price-fixing intervention when price fluctuations occur due to market forces alone; Al-Hisbah is empowered specifically against undue monopolistic and unjust pricing/production/distribution practices.","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.4","topicLabel":"Positive versus Normative Economics in the Islamic Framework","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish positive (economics proper) from normative (welfare) economics in the Islamic framework","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, the State should always fix prices to protect consumers","No — the Prophet discouraged price-fixing intervention when fluctuations arise from genuine market forces alone; Al-Hisbah intervenes only against undue monopolistic/unjust practices","Yes, but only for luxury goods","The State has no role in markets under any circumstances"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to S.M. Hasanuz Zaman's definition (cited by Ayub), what is Islamic economics?","explanation":"Hasanuz Zaman defines Islamic economics as the knowledge of applying Shari'ah injunctions/rules that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources, enabling individuals to fulfil their obligations to Allah and society.","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","topicLabel":"Defining Islamic Economics: Scholarly Definitions and the Integrated Model","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish positive (economics proper) from normative (welfare) economics in the Islamic framework","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[31]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The scientific study of consumer preferences","The knowledge of applying Shari'ah injunctions/rules that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources","A purely mathematical model of resource allocation","A description of pre-modern trade routes"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-16","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the eight criteria of the 'integrated model' of the Islamic social framework?","explanation":"The eight criteria include equilibrium between work and worship, human equality, mutual responsibilities, distributive justice, balanced use of God's bounties, limited sovereignty of self-interest, co-existence, and freedom of conscience. 'Maximization of shareholder value' is a conventional corporate finance concept, not part of this list.","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","topicLabel":"Defining Islamic Economics: Scholarly Definitions and the Integrated Model","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[32]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Equilibrium between work and worship","Human equality","Maximization of shareholder value","Distributive justice","Freedom of conscience"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4]},{"id":"q-ch2-17","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Islamic economics places a ceiling (maximum limit) on the quantity of wealth an individual may lawfully hold.","explanation":"False. Islam limits wealth acquisition by quality/means (lawful, Halal methods), not by quantity — there is no ceiling on the amount of wealth an individual may lawfully accumulate.","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","topicLabel":"Ownership of Resources and Property Rights in Islamic Economics","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch2-18","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are recognized means of legitimately acquiring property in Islamic economics, per Chapter 2?","explanation":"Recognized means are work, inheritance, purchasing/obtaining for sustenance, gifts, and State grants of possession. Gambling winnings are not a recognized legitimate means (gambling/Maisir is prohibited).","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","topicLabel":"Ownership of Resources and Property Rights in Islamic Economics","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Work","Inheritance","Gambling winnings","Gifts","State grants of possession"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4]},{"id":"q-ch2-19","type":"scenario","prompt":"An elderly, disabled individual has no income and no relatives or neighbours able to support them. Per Chapter 2's cascading welfare obligation, who bears ultimate responsibility?","explanation":"The support obligation cascades from the individual, to relatives/neighbours, to the State as the ultimate guarantor, especially for the disabled and destitute, per the cited Hadith.","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","topicLabel":"The Islamic Welfare Approach: Need Fulfilment and Falah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No one — Islamic economics has no such mechanism","The individual's distant acquaintances only","The State, per the Hadith: 'The Imam (ruler) is in-charge and responsible for his citizens'","Only religious charities, never the government"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch2-20","type":"short","prompt":"What gap does Chapra identify in Islamic economics regarding macro/microeconomic modeling?","explanation":"Chapra notes the lack of a rigorous model connecting Islamic values and institutions to measurable macro/micro outcomes — a major ongoing challenge for the field.","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","topicLabel":"The Islamic Welfare Approach: Need Fulfilment and Falah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[35]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"There is no theoretical macroeconomic model showing how Islamic values/institutions and different economic/social/political sectors interact to realize the vision; microeconomics is a particularly underdeveloped area, with little established relationship between macro goals and individual economic agents' behaviour."},{"id":"q-ch2-21","type":"match","prompt":"Match each capitalist factor of production to its conventional compensation: Capital, Land, Labour, Entrepreneur.","explanation":"In conventional (capitalist) economics: Capital→interest, Land→rent, Labour→wages, Entrepreneur/organization→profit.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","topicLabel":"Factors of Production: Islamic Economics versus Capitalism","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"pairs":[["Capital","Interest"],["Land","Rent"],["Labour","Wages"],["Entrepreneur","Profit"]]},{"id":"q-ch2-22","type":"scenario","prompt":"An investor provides money capital to a business venture, expecting a profit share. The business incurs a loss at year-end. Per Chapter 2's Islamic framework for profit-sharing capital, what happens to the investor's capital?","explanation":"Since money-capital in Islamic economics only earns a residual, risk-contingent profit (not a guaranteed return), a business loss pro-rata erodes the capital provider's principal — they must accept the shortfall.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","topicLabel":"Factors of Production: Islamic Economics versus Capitalism","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[36]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The investor is guaranteed the return of their full principal regardless of the loss","The investor's capital is pro-rata eroded by the loss — they accept the shortfall since money-capital cannot earn a risk-free return","The manager must personally cover the investor's loss from their own funds","The government reimburses the investor automatically"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-23","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can land, but not money, earn a fixed rental in the Islamic framework described in Chapter 2?","explanation":"Land as a factor of production retains its corpus/original form during use (so it can be leased for a fixed rental), whereas money/capital is consumed or changes form when used, so it cannot fetch rent — only a risk-contingent profit.","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","topicLabel":"Factors of Production: Islamic Economics versus Capitalism","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how ownership, welfare, and the factors of production differ in Islamic economics versus capitalism","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[36]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Land is more valuable than money","Land's corpus/original form remains unaltered when used, while money is consumed or changes form when used","Money is prohibited from all transactions","There is no difference; both can earn fixed rental"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per Chapter 2, why is secular State intervention alone not a sufficient fix to the flaws of laissez-faire capitalism?","explanation":"The author argues that secular State intervention, absent ethical/moral grounding, still allows the profit motive to find loopholes for corruption, and can even legitimize harmful industries (e.g. gambling) as public policy.","topicId":"t2.5.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","topicLabel":"Restrained Individual Freedom: Critique of Laissez-Faire and Secular State Intervention","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because governments never have enough money to intervene","Because without ethical norms, the profit motive still finds loopholes for injustice/corruption, and can even legitimize socially harmful industries as public policy","Because Islamic economics rejects all forms of government","Because secular States always increase interest rates"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-25","type":"multi","prompt":"Which three prohibitions does Chapter 2 identify as the most important in the economic sphere?","explanation":"The three most important economic prohibitions per Chapter 2 are interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar), and gambling/games of chance (Maisir/Qimar), due to their extremely harmful social impact.","topicId":"t2.5.4","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","topicLabel":"Restrained Individual Freedom: Critique of Laissez-Faire and Secular State Intervention","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37,38]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Interest (Riba)","Advertising","Hazard (Gharar)","Gambling/games of chance (Maisir/Qimar)","Partnership (Musharakah)"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch2-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does Ayub argue the Pareto efficiency concept is unsuitable as a framework for Islamic economics?","explanation":"Ayub argues Pareto efficiency rests on unrealistic assumptions (harmony of individual/social interest, equal income/wealth distribution, perfect competition, prices reflecting true wants) that don't hold in real markets, creating a built-in bias against normative (justice-oriented) goals.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Pareto efficiency requires government to own all industry","Because it assumes conditions (perfect competition, equal wealth distribution, prices reflecting true wants) that no real-world market satisfies, biasing outcomes against normative goals","Because Pareto efficiency was invented after Islamic economics","Because it is only applicable to agricultural economies"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-27","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among Chapra's five listed functions of the Islamic State in economics and finance?","explanation":"Chapra's five functions: poverty eradication/full employment/growth; economic planning; social/economic justice; monetary value stability; and international relations/defence. Nationalizing all private industry is not among them.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[40,41]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Eradication of poverty, full employment, optimum growth","Nationalizing all private industry","Ensuring social and economic justice","Stability in the value of money","Harmonizing international relations and national defence"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3,4]},{"id":"q-ch2-28","type":"short","prompt":"Why does Chapter 2 treat continuous erosion of money's real value as a matter of social justice, not just monetary policy?","explanation":"The chapter frames monetary stability as grounded in the Qur'anic injunction to give full measure and weight without defrauding — currency erosion is treated as a justice issue, not merely a technical monetary concern.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[40,41]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because money is a measure of value; per Qur'an 6:152 and 7:85 ('give full measure and weight'), continuous, significant erosion of money's real value is interpreted as 'corrupting the world' due to its adverse effect on social justice and general welfare."},{"id":"q-ch2-29","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the eight basic socio-economic rights Ayub identifies from the Qur'an and Sunnah?","explanation":"The eight rights are: safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, satisfaction of basic needs, redress, education, and a healthy environment. A fixed interest rate and unlimited borrowing are not among them and conflict with Islamic economic principles.","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","topicLabel":"Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: Primary and Secondary","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the primary (Maqasid) and secondary objectives of Shari'ah and explain their relevance to economics","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[25]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The right to safety","The right to a fixed interest rate","The right to be informed","The right to satisfaction of basic needs","The right to unlimited borrowing"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch2-30","type":"short","prompt":"What does Maurice Lombard's 'The Golden Age of Islam' say unified the medieval Muslim world economically, and what ended that unity?","explanation":"Lombard credits the Dinar and Arabic with economically unifying the medieval Muslim world, describing its fragmentation into regional civilizations after 11th-century crises.","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","topicLabel":"Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify key classical Muslim scholars (e.g. Ibn Khaldun, Ibn Taymiyah) and their economic contributions","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[28,29]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The Muslim Dinar (one main currency) and Arabic (one international commercial language) unified it; crises, disturbances and invasions in the late 11th century fragmented the unified Muslim civilization into regional particularisms (Turkish, Persian, Syrian, Egyptian, Maghreb Islam)."},{"id":"q-ch2-31","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per Chapter 2, obedience to Islamic State rulers is unconditional and absolute, regardless of what they command.","explanation":"False. Obedience to rulers is conditional - per the cited Hadith, 'Obedience (to rulers) is not valid where a disobedience to Allah is involved.'","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch2-32","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trader in an Islamic market sells a commodity at a price far below the prevailing market rate, distorting fair competition. Based on Caliph Umar Farooq's precedent cited in Chapter 2, what State action would be appropriate?","explanation":"Caliph Umar Farooq asked a trader selling well below market price to raise the price or leave the market - an example of permissible State intervention to protect fair market functioning against distortion, not routine price-fixing.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39,40]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No action - the State must never interfere with any pricing decision","The State may ask the trader to raise the price to the market rate or leave the market, to protect fair market functioning","The State should nationalize the trader's business","The State should fine only the trader's customers"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch2-33","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are among the four types of government economic action identified in the Islamic economics literature (Chapter 2)?","explanation":"The four types are: ensuring compliance with the Islamic code of conduct; maintaining healthy market conditions; modifying resource allocation/distribution via regulation or intervention; and taking positive steps in production/capital formation. Nationalizing all banks and abolishing taxation are not among them.","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","topicLabel":"Liberalism versus State Intervention: The State's Active Economic Role","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the State's role in an Islamic economy and why the author rejects unrestrained laissez-faire and Pareto efficiency as adequate frameworks","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39,40]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ensuring compliance with the Islamic code of conduct through education and, if necessary, compulsion","Nationalizing all privately owned banks","Maintaining healthy market conditions","Taking positive steps in production/capital formation to accelerate growth","Abolishing all forms of taxation"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch2-34","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per Ayub, what specific effect did the Enlightenment's secularist worldview have on economics and society?","explanation":"Ayub argues the Enlightenment treated revealed religious truths as mere inventions, weakening religion's hold and the collective moral sanction it provided, depriving society of morally-oriented filtering, motivating and restructuring mechanisms.","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"section":"2.3","topicLabel":"Why Study Islamic Economics? The Integrated Islamic Worldview","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why the author argues Islamic economics should be studied","source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[26]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It strengthened religion's collective moral sanction on economic behaviour","It weakened religion's hold and its collective moral sanction, depriving society of morally-oriented filtering and restructuring mechanisms","It had no discernible effect on economic thought","It immediately eliminated global poverty"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f2.1","front":"Ibahatul Asliyah","back":"Rule of General Permissibility: in socio-economic matters, all acts and things not expressly prohibited by the original sources are permissible.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.2","front":"Primary and secondary sources of Shari’ah","back":"Primary: Qur’an and Sunnah. Secondary: Ijma‘a (consensus) and Qiyas (analogy), both based on Ijtihad. Tools: Maslaha-e-Mursalah and ‘Urf.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.3","front":"Six primary objectives (Maqasid) of Shari’ah","back":"Protection and preservation of religion, life, progeny (family), property, intellect and honour.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["fc-ch2-4"],"backAlt":"Protection/preservation of: Religion, Life, Progeny (family), Property, Intellect, and Honour."},{"id":"f2.4","front":"Secondary objectives of Shari’ah","back":"Justice and equity; social security, mutual help and solidarity; peace and security; cooperation in goodness and prohibition of evil; universal moral values and preservation of nature.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.5","front":"Hisbah","back":"The institution of the ombudsman, empowered to check monopolistic and unjust pricing, production and distribution practices.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.6","front":"Islamic economics — Hasanuz Zaman’s definition","back":"Knowledge of application of Shari’ah injunctions and rules that stop injustice in the acquisition and disposition of material resources, to satisfy individuals and enable them to perform obligations to Allah and society.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[31,32]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.7","front":"Reward of capital: capitalism vs Islamic economics","back":"Capitalism: interest. Islamic economics: profit as a residual, with liability for loss; money cannot earn rent or profit in itself.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.8","front":"Five factors of production suggested by the author","back":"Land, capital, labour, management and responsibility/liability.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.9","front":"Dhaman","back":"Taking responsibility/liability — a basis (with effort and work) of entitlement to income and profit.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33,34]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.10","front":"Falah","back":"Welfare in this world and the Hereafter — the goal of the Islamic welfare function.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.11","front":"Most important prohibitions in economics (Ch. 2)","back":"Interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar) and gambling (Maisir) — because of their extremely harmful impacts on society.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t2.5.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37,38]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.12","front":"Four types of government action in economic life","back":"Ensure compliance with the Islamic code; maintain healthy market conditions; modify allocation/distribution; take positive steps in production and capital formation.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.13","front":"Israf","back":"Wasteful consumption — avoided in an ideal Islamic economy.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5","pages":[32,33]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.14","front":"Positive vs normative economics","back":"Positive: value-neutral, describes what is. Normative/welfare: involves value judgements about what should be. Islamic economics is normative (and positive).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.15","front":"Maslaha-e-Mursalah and ‘Urf","back":"General welfare/interest and prevalent practice — tools jurists use when deciding Shari’ah positions, without compromising the Qur’an and Sunnah.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.16","front":"Eight socio-economic rights (Ch. 2)","back":"Safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, basic needs, redress, education, healthy environment.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.17","front":"Mannan’s seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics","back":"Ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[25,27]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["fc-ch2-8"],"backAlt":"Ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, and international."},{"id":"f2.18","front":"Ibn Khaldun’s anticipations","back":"Adam Smith (labour value), Malthus (population), Keynes (normative role of the State); also a theory of growth — “founder of economic science”.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,30]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.19","front":"Equal vs equitable distribution","back":"Islam aims at equitable, not equal, distribution — like blood reaching body organs according to their functions.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.20","front":"Key macro features of an ideal Islamic economy","back":"No Riba, real trade, profit-sharing, Zakat, avoidance of Israf, effective State oversight.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5","pages":[32,33]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.21","front":"Chapra’s functions of the Islamic State","back":"Poverty eradication and full employment with growth; planning; socio-economic justice; stable money value; international relations and defence.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f2.22","front":"Umar Farooq and the under-pricing trader","back":"He asked a trader selling far below the market price to raise his price or leave the market — the State may curb practices that harm others.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-1","front":"Define Shari'ah and name its five branches.","back":"Shari'ah is divine guidance from the Qur'an and Sunnah, regulating individual and collective human conduct. Its branches: Aqaid (belief/worship), Akhlaq (self-discipline), Ahkam (socio-economic/legal systems), Fraidh (obligations), Nawahi (prohibitions).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2","pages":[21]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-2","front":"What are the primary vs secondary sources of Shari'ah?","back":"Primary: the Qur'an and Sunnah (revelation). Secondary: Ijma'a (consensus) and Qiyas (analogy), both derived through Ijtihad, supplemented by Maslaha-e-Mursalah (public interest) and 'Urf (custom).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-3","front":"State the principle of 'General Permissibility' (Ibahatul Asliyah).","back":"In socio-economic (Mu'amalat) matters, all acts and things not expressly prohibited by the original sources of Shari'ah are permissible.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-5","front":"List the five Secondary Objectives of Shari'ah.","back":"Establishing justice/equity; promoting social security, mutual help and solidarity; maintaining peace/security; promoting cooperation in goodness and prohibiting evil; promoting universal moral values and preservation of nature.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[24]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-6","front":"Define Falah and contrast it with 'pleasure' as used in conventional economics.","back":"Falah is welfare/success in this world and the Hereafter — the Islamic concept of true happiness. It differs from mere 'pleasure', the objective of conventional (positive) economics.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[23]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-7","front":"Why, per Ayub, does Islam not separate the 'pious person' from the 'worldly person'?","back":"Because Islam integrates devotion with guidance on economy, politics and international relations; Qur'an 2:177 frames righteousness as including both belief and economic acts like spending on kin, orphans and the needy.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[25,26]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-9","front":"What did Ibn Khaldun's Muqaddamah argue about State involvement in commerce?","back":"It strongly supported free private enterprise, opposed government interference in the market mechanism (via inequitable taxation or direct participation), and warned that a State monopoly in commerce/agriculture ruins the economy.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-10","front":"Which three later Western economists is Ibn Khaldun said to have anticipated, and for which ideas?","back":"Adam Smith (labour value theory), Malthus (population theory), and Keynes (a normative role for the State) — plus a coherent theory of economic growth.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[27,28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-11","front":"Define Thaman-e-mithl and who developed the concept.","back":"Normal/market price that has to be paid when an issue arises in deciding the actual price in a transaction; the concept was developed by Ibn Taymiyah.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[29]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-12","front":"Distinguish positive economics from normative economics, per Lionel Robbins and Ayub's framing.","back":"Positive economics (per Robbins) is value-neutral and descriptive (describes but doesn't prescribe). Normative/welfare economics necessarily involves value judgements and is prescriptive. Islamic economics is normative.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-13","front":"Define Al-Hisbah and when it is invoked.","back":"The institution of the ombudsman — a social regulatory body empowered to check market imbalances. It is invoked specifically against undue monopolistic and unjust pricing/production/distribution practices, not against ordinary market-driven price fluctuations.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-14","front":"Islamic economics aims for 'equitable' distribution rather than 'equal' distribution. Explain the analogy Ayub uses.","back":"He compares it to blood, which is not distributed equally among the body's organs, because each organ has a different function and need — distribution should be fair/appropriate to role and need, not identical.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-15","front":"How does Mohsin S. Khan (IMF) define Islamic economics?","back":"A complete system prescribing a specific pattern of social/economic behaviour, covering property rights, incentives, resource allocation, economic freedom, decision-making and the government's role — with social justice and specific income/wealth distribution patterns as the overriding objective.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[31]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-16","front":"List the eight criteria of the 'integrated model' of the Islamic social framework.","back":"Equilibrium between work and worship; human equality; mutual responsibilities in society; distributive justice; balanced/beneficent use of God's bounties; limited sovereignty of individual self-interest; the principle of co-existence; freedom of conscience.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.4.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4.1","pages":[32]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-17","front":"Does Islam limit wealth acquisition by quantity or by quality/means? Explain.","back":"By quality/means, not quantity — wealth must be lawfully (Halal) acquired, but there is no ceiling on how much one may hold. This preserves the incentive to work diligently while preventing greed-driven corruption of the economy.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-18","front":"What Qur'anic verses (24:33, 57:7) does Ayub cite to establish that human ownership is a trust from Allah?","back":"24:33 - 'And give them from the Mal of Allah, which He gave to you.' 57:7 - 'And spend from what He put you in charge of.' Both frame human wealth as held in trust from Allah, not owned absolutely.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.1","pages":[33]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-19","front":"What is the cascading obligation to support someone who cannot meet their own basic needs?","back":"First, the individual is expected to work and self-support. If unable, relatives/neighbours are obligated to help. If no one can, the State must step in — per the Hadith, 'The Imam (ruler) is in-charge and responsible for his citizens.'","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.2","pages":[34,35]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-20","front":"Compare capitalism's and Islamic economics' four factors of production and their rewards.","back":"Capitalism: Capital→interest, Land→rent, Labour→wages, Entrepreneur→profit. Islamic economics: money-capital cannot earn interest/rent (consumed/altered on use) — it earns only a residual, risk-contingent 'profit'; Land is rentable (form unaltered); Labour earns wages.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,36]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-21","front":"How does a Qard (loan) differ from profit-sharing capital in terms of loss treatment?","back":"A Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal amount regardless of the borrower's profit or loss. Profit-sharing capital, by contrast, is pro-rata eroded by any business loss — the capital provider accepts the shortfall.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[36]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-22","front":"What are the most important economic prohibitions in Islamic economics, per Chapter 2, and why?","back":"Interest (Riba), hazard (Gharar), and gambling/games of chance (Maisir/Qimar) — due to their extremely harmful impact on society, undermining the overall Maqasid of Shari'ah.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t2.5.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.4","pages":[37,38]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-23","front":"Why does Ayub reject the conventional 'Pareto efficiency' concept as a fit for Islamic economics?","back":"Because it rests on assumptions (perfect competition, equal income/wealth distribution, prices truly reflecting wants) that no real-world market satisfies, creating a built-in bias against normative goals if allocation relies primarily on prices.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-24","front":"Summarize the Prophet's 'boat parable' and its economic lesson.","back":"Two groups share a boat; those in the hold want to drill a hole to fetch water more easily. If those on the upper deck do not stop them, all drown; if they do, all are saved. Lesson: society cannot remain a silent spectator to harmful individual economic acts.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,39]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-25","front":"List Chapra's five functions of the Islamic State in economics and finance.","back":"1) Poverty eradication, full employment, optimum growth; 2) Economic planning; 3) Ensuring social/economic justice; 4) Stability in the value of money; 5) Harmonizing international relations and national defence.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[40,41]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-26","front":"List the eight basic socio-economic rights of human beings that Ayub identifies from the Qur'an and Sunnah.","back":"The right to: safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, satisfaction of basic needs, redress, education, and a healthy environment.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[25]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-27","front":"According to Maurice Lombard's 'The Golden Age of Islam' (cited by Ayub), what role did the medieval Muslim world play in trade and culture, and what unified it?","back":"The Muslim East was the 'driving force' behind economic and cultural life while the West was a relative 'void'. The Muslim world was unified by one main currency (the Dinar) and one international commercial language (Arabic).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-28","front":"Per Maurice Lombard, what happened to the unified Muslim world after the late 11th century?","back":"Crises, disturbances and invasions in the second half of the 11th century impeded trade and provoked urban decline; the single Muslim civilization fragmented into regional particularisms - Turkish, Persian, Syrian, Egyptian and Maghreb Islam.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[28,29]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-29","front":"What does Ayub argue about the Enlightenment's effect on religion's role in economics?","back":"The Enlightenment's secularist worldview treated revealed religious truths as mere human inventions, weakening religion's hold and the collective moral sanction it provides, depriving society of morally-oriented filtering and restructuring mechanisms.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3","pages":[26]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-30","front":"What historical example does Ayub give of an Islamic State intervening in market pricing, and why was it permissible?","back":"Caliph Umar Farooq asked a trader selling a commodity well below the market price to either raise the price or leave the market - permissible because it protected fair market functioning against a distortion, not because it was routine price-fixing.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39,40]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-31","front":"Under what condition, per Chapter 2, does obedience to rulers cease to be required?","back":"Obedience to rulers is not valid where it would involve disobedience to Allah - per the Hadith, 'Obedience (to rulers) is not valid where a disobedience to Allah is involved.'","category":"Principles","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-32","front":"List the four types of government economic action identified in the Islamic economics literature, per Chapter 2.","back":"1) Ensuring compliance with the Islamic code of conduct (education, compulsion if needed); 2) maintaining healthy market conditions; 3) modifying resource allocation/income distribution via guidance/regulation or direct intervention; 4) taking positive steps in production/capital formation to accelerate growth.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[39,40]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch2-33","front":"What did Ibn Khaldun notice about productivity, market extent, and division of labour - anticipating which later economist?","back":"Like Adam Smith, Ibn Khaldun observed that productivity depends on the extent of the market, division of labour and specialization.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t2.3.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.3.1","pages":[28]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e2.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the objectives (Maqasid) of Shari’ah and discuss their relevance for Islamic finance.","expectedStructure":["Define Shari’ah and Maqasid","Explain the overall objective: well-being in this world and the Hereafter","List and briefly explain the six primary objectives","List the secondary objectives","Relate protection of property to the law of Mu‘amalat and finance","Mention socio-economic rights and the State’s protective role"],"keyConcepts":["Maqasid","stewardship","Mu‘amalat","justice"],"essentialPoints":["Identified by Ghazali, Shatbi, Ibn Ashoor via inductive survey","Religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour","Justice, social security, peace, cooperation, moral values","Halal earning; discourage concentration of wealth"],"commonMistakes":["Mixing primary and secondary objectives","Omitting the link to finance"],"topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,25]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e2.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between the treatment of factors of production in capitalism and in Islamic economics.","expectedStructure":["Table of factors vs rewards","Explain capital’s treatment (profit with liability)","Explain why money cannot earn rent","Five factors in the Islamic view"],"keyConcepts":["capital","rent","residual profit","liability"],"essentialPoints":["Capital–interest vs profit","Land–rent in both","Labour–wages in both","Entrepreneur profit vs responsibility-based profit"],"commonMistakes":["Stating that Islam denies any return to capital"],"topicId":"t2.5.3","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.3","pages":[35,37]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e2.3","kind":"short","prompt":"What are the sources of Shari’ah? Explain the rule of General Permissibility.","expectedStructure":["Primary sources","Secondary sources","Juristic tools","Rights of Allah vs rights of human beings","Ibahatul Asliyah"],"keyConcepts":["Qur’an","Sunnah","Ijma‘a","Qiyas","Ijtihad"],"essentialPoints":["Everything not expressly prohibited is permissible in Mu‘amalat"],"commonMistakes":["Listing Ijtihad as a primary source"],"topicId":"t2.2.1","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.1","pages":[21,22]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e2.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"“Islamic economics accepts the market but not laissez-faire.” Discuss.","expectedStructure":["Market concomitants accepted","Critique of laissez-faire and Pareto optimality","Hisbah and price fixing","Four government actions / Chapra’s State functions"],"keyConcepts":["Hisbah","State role","equitable distribution"],"essentialPoints":["No price fixing under genuine market forces","Intervention for monopolies and injustice","Stable value of money"],"commonMistakes":["Claiming Islam favours central planning"],"topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e2.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"What is the difference between an equal and an equitable distribution in Islamic economics?","expectedStructure":["Define both","Blood-and-organs analogy"],"keyConcepts":["equity"],"essentialPoints":["Equitable: according to role/need, with equal chances to earn"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Islam requires equal wealth for all"],"topicId":"t2.4","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.4","pages":[30,31]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e2.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the role of the State in an Islamic economy. How does it differ from laissez-faire and from secular State intervention?","expectedStructure":["Markets accepted, laissez-faire rejected","Why secular intervention falls short","Four types of government action","Chapra’s five functions of the State","Hisbah and precedents (Umar, price intervention)","Conclusion: overseer and facilitator"],"keyConcepts":["Hisbah","Pareto optimality","restrained freedom","socio-economic justice","stable money value"],"essentialPoints":["No intervention in genuine market price movements","Act against monopoly and distortion","Recover usurped wealth","Real-sector return as benchmark"],"commonMistakes":["Saying Islam wants a command economy","Ignoring individual freedom","Omitting Hisbah"],"topicId":"t2.5.5","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.5.5","pages":[38,41]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-20","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah) and how they shape Islamic economics.","expectedStructure":["Define Maqasid al-Shari'ah and its role in guiding permissibility/prohibition via Ijtihad and Qiyas","List the primary objectives (protection of religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour)","Note the secondary objectives (justice, social security, peace, cooperation in goodness)","Explain how these objectives translate into economic principles, e.g. protection of property underlying the law of Mu'amalat"],"keyConcepts":["Maqasid al-Shari'ah","Primary objectives","Secondary objectives","Mu'amalat"],"essentialPoints":["Maqasid al-Shari'ah are the objectives Shari'ah tends to realize, identified by jurists such as Ghazali, Shatbi and Tahir ibne Ashoor through an inductive survey of the Qur'an and Sunnah, and they help jurists determine prohibition or permissibility of matters via Ijtihad and Qiyas.","The primary objectives are protection and preservation of religion, life, progeny (the family unit), property, intellect, and honour; protection of property specifically underlies Islam's comprehensive law of Mu'amalat (transactions among members of society).","Secondary objectives include establishing justice and equity in society, promoting social security and mutual help for the poor and needy, maintaining peace and security, and promoting cooperation in matters of goodness.","The overall objective of Shari'ah behind these injunctions is the well-being (Falah) of human beings in this world and the Hereafter, distinct from positive economics' pleasure-maximization goal, and this is what orients Islamic economics towards socio-economic and distributive justice rather than growth for its own sake."],"commonMistakes":["Treating Maqasid as an abstract list disconnected from actual rules — the book ties protection of property directly to the law of Mu'amalat and protection of progeny to marriage/family rules.","Confusing primary and secondary objectives, or omitting that jurists derive contract validity/prohibition judgments by reference to these objectives."],"topicId":"t2.2.2","chapter":2,"source":{"chapter":2,"section":"2.2.2","pages":[22,23,24]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
